{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04532","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04532","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04532","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xurui","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Xurui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuo","familyName":"Huai","name":"Huai, Shuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"JingJing","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, JingJing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiayi","familyName":"Kong","name":"Kong, Jiayi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"More Than Meets the Eye? Uncovering the Reasoning-Planning Disconnect in Training Vision-Language Driving Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T06:50:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:17:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-08T02:08:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-09T00:26:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-Language Model (VLM) driving agents promise explainable end-to-end autonomy by first producing natural-language reasoning and then predicting trajectory planning. However, whether planning is causally driven by this reasoning remains a critical but unverified assumption. To investigate this, we build DriveMind, a large-scale driving Visual Question Answering corpus with plan-aligned Chain-of-Thought (CoT), automatically generated from nuPlan. Our data generation process converts sensors and annotations into structured inputs and, crucially, separates priors from to-be-reasoned signals, enabling clean information ablations. Using DriveMind, we train representative VLM agents with Supervised Fine-Tuning and Group Relative Policy Optimization and evaluate them with nuPlan's metrics. Our results, unfortunately, indicate a consistent causal disconnect in reasoning-planning: removing ego/navigation priors causes large drops in planning scores, whereas removing CoT produces only minor changes. Attention analysis further shows that planning primarily focuses on priors rather than the CoT. Based on this evidence, we propose the Reasoning-Planning Decoupling Hypothesis, positing that the training-yielded reasoning is an ancillary byproduct rather than a causal mediator. To enable efficient diagnosis, we introduce a novel, training-free probe that measures an agent's reliance on priors by evaluating its planning robustness against minor input perturbations. In summary, we provide the community with a new dataset and a diagnostic tool to evaluate the causal fidelity of future models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"NeurIPS 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04532","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:16:48Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:16:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-09T03:15:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04226","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04226","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04226","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dustin","familyName":"Wright","name":"Wright, Dustin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sarah","familyName":"Masud","name":"Masud, Sarah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jared","familyName":"Moore","name":"Moore, Jared","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Srishti","familyName":"Yadav","name":"Yadav, Srishti","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maria","familyName":"Antoniak","name":"Antoniak, Maria","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peter Ebert","familyName":"Christensen","name":"Christensen, Peter Ebert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chan Young","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Chan Young","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Isabelle","familyName":"Augenstein","name":"Augenstein, Isabelle","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"What and Whose Knowledge? Measuring Epistemic Diversity in Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T14:29:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:58:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T16:07:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:57:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-08T07:35:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:30:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-30T14:52:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10-31T00:51:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-11-11T18:13:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-11-12T02:03:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-01-28T13:27:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2026-01-29T01:45:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2026-08-31T12:37:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v7"},{"date":"2026-09-01T02:02:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v7"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"7","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly used as primary knowledge sources, yet their epistemic diversity - defined as the diversity of real-world claims in their outputs - has never been measured. Low epistemic diversity would pose a risk of knowledge collapse as homogeneous LLMs mediate a shrinking in the range of accessible information over time. The dominant paradigm is that overall LLM diversity is low, but this is always with respect to a single point in time, with no reference baseline or consideration for variation across countries. We address this gap in knowledge by performing the first systematic study of epistemic diversity in LLMs across time and cultural context, testing 27 LLMs on 155 topics covering 12 countries, resulting in 1.7M responses and 70M individual claims. We find that epistemic diversity has increased substantially over the past three years, a positive counter to recent diversity pessimism. However, despite progress, we find that every system is less diverse than a search baseline. This gap is not uniform: RAG can improve diversity, while large models are counterintuitively less diverse than smaller ones. Moreover, LLM parametric knowledge systematically reflects English over local-language knowledge for country specific topics. Together, these results demonstrate that while progress on epistemic diversity is tangible, it is insufficient and unevenly distributed."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to EMNLP 2026; 18 pages, 8 figures, 4 tables; v2: Fixed the modeling for table 3, random effect is the model version; v3: Fixed minor formatting issues in tables 2 and 3; v4: Fixed some typos and model description; v5: Updated metadata; v6: Improved search baseline, writing revisions, added comparisons to semantic similarity only approaches; v7 changelog: EMNLP camera ready"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04226","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":6,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:09:20Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:09:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-01T05:01:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03536","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03536","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03536","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaohan","familyName":"Meng","name":"Meng, Zhaohan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zaiqiao","familyName":"Meng","name":"Meng, Zaiqiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siwei","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Siwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ke","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Ke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Iadh","familyName":"Ounis","name":"Ounis, Iadh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GraphMed-LT: Patient-Specific Graph Memory with Latent Clinical Thought Refinement for Multi-Turn Medical Conversations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T22:11:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:12:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-14T11:40:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-15T00:44:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-22T23:26:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-25T00:43:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multi-turn medical question answering (QA) aims to model realistic clinical diagnosis, where a doctor gathers patient information across multiple turns of conversation. Existing multi-turn medical conversation systems have shown promising progress, but they often rely on accumulated conversation histories as memory, leaving clinical evidence fragmented across turns. We propose GraphMed-LT, a patient-specific graph memory approach with latent clinical thought refinement for multi-turn medical conversations. GraphMed-LT extracts patient-specific clinical triplets from patient responses, retrieves relevant knowledge triplets, and organises them into an incrementally updated graph memory. The graph memory is projected into graph-conditioned evidence tokens and refined inside a trainable doctor agent through hidden-state feedback, enabling the agent to update its internal clinical context before asking follow-up questions or producing the final answer. Experiments on three multi-turn medical QA benchmarks show that GraphMed-LT consistently outperforms existing multi-turn medical conversation baselines across multiple LLM backbones, achieving up to a 6.3 percentage-point absolute improvement over the strongest baseline. Further analyses show that GraphMed-LT asks more answerable follow-up questions and provides consistent gains across medical specialties."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to EMNLP 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03536","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:52:18Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:52:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-25T03:50:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04465","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04465","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04465","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiping","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhiping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi Evie","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yi Evie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Freda","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Freda","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianshi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Tianshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Autonomy Reshapes How Personalization Affects Privacy Concerns and Trust in LLM Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T03:38:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:13:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-06T00:31:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-07T01:07:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-09T12:40:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-11T01:04:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"LLM agents require personal information for personalization in order to effectively act on users' behalf, but this raises privacy concerns that can discourage data sharing, limiting both the autonomy levels at which agents can operate and the effectiveness of personalization. Yet the expanded design space of agent autonomy also presents opportunities to shape these effects, which remain underexplored. We conducted a $3\\times3$ between-subjects experiment ($N=450$) to study how agent autonomy level influences personalization's effects on users' privacy concerns, trust, and willingness to use, as well as the underlying psychological processes. We find that risk-contingent autonomy, where the agent delegates control back to users upon detecting potential privacy leakage, improves users' perceived control. This in turn attenuates personalization's adverse effects: privacy concerns rise less and trust declines less. Our results suggest that designing $\\textbf{agent's autonomy}$ that supports $\\textbf{human autonomy}$ (both perceived control and oversight effectiveness) helps users benefit from personalization without being deterred by growing privacy concerns. This positions aligning decision authority between users and agents ($\\textbf{autonomy alignment}$) as an important opportunity and challenge for trustworthy human-agent interaction."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to COLM 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04465","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:15:11Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:15:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-11T04:12:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04520","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04520","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04520","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Hanyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruohan","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Ruohan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yutong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yutong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guoxiong","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Guoxiong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xintao","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Xintao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Aria: An Agent For Retrieval and Iterative Auto-Formalization via Dependency Graph"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T06:25:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:16:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-02T09:05:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-03T00:37:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Accurate auto-formalization of theorem statements is essential for advancing automated discovery and verification of research-level mathematics, yet remains a major bottleneck for LLMs due to hallucinations, semantic mismatches, and their inability to synthesize new definitions. To tackle these issues, we present Aria (Agent for Retrieval and Iterative Autoformalization), a system for conjecture-level formalization in Lean that emulates human expert reasoning via a two-phase Graph-of-Thought process: recursively decomposing statements into a dependency graph and then constructing formalizations from grounded concepts. To ensure semantic correctness, we introduce AriaScorer, a checker that retrieves definitions from Mathlib for term-level grounding, enabling rigorous and reliable verification. We evaluate Aria on diverse benchmarks. On ProofNet, it achieves 91.6% compilation success rate and 68.5% final accuracy, surpassing previous methods. On FATE-X, a suite of challenging algebra problems from research literature, it outperforms the best baseline with 44.0% vs. 24.0% final accuracy. On a dataset of homological conjectures, Aria reaches 42.9% final accuracy while all other models score 0%."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04520","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:16:31Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:16:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-03T02:13:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04391","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04391","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04391","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saurabh","familyName":"Ranjan","name":"Ranjan, Saurabh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brian","familyName":"Odegaard","name":"Odegaard, Brian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Psychological Imagination Networks Show Cross-Population Centrality and Clustering Alignment in Humans That Large Language Models Fail to Replicate"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Social and Information Networks (cs.SI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Neurons and Cognition (q-bio.NC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T23:01:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:08:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-06T19:24:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-10T01:02:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-07T07:07:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-09T01:45:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-10T02:12:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-02-11T01:18:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-07-01T18:39:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-07-03T00:05:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Mental imagery vividness is a stable individual trait, yet whether imagined scenarios share relational structure across human and synthetic large language model (LLM) populations remains unknown. We applied psychological network analysis to vividness ratings from two validated questionnaires: the Vividness of Visual Imagery Questionnaire (VVIQ-2) and the Plymouth Sensory Imagery Questionnaire (PSIQ), across geographically and linguistically distinct human samples (Florida, Poland, and London; total N = 2,743) and six large language models (LLMs; Gemma3-12B/27B, their quantization-aware counterparts, Llama3.3-70B, and Llama4-16x17B). Imagination networks were constructed as regularized partial correlation graphs, with node centrality and community structure compared across populations using Pearson correlations and the Adjusted Rand Index (ARI). Human networks showed robust cross-population centrality correlations for expected influence, strength, and closeness (r = 0.31-0.93), and community detection recovered clusters aligned with VVIQ-2 scene contexts (ARI = 0.27-0.40) and PSIQ sensory modalities (ARI = 0.87-1.0). Betweenness centrality was unstable across all populations, consistent with its sensitivity to individual experiential history. LLMs failed to replicate human network structure: LLM-human centrality correlations were weak and largely non-significant after correction, and most LLM configurations produced degenerate single-cluster topologies (median ARI = 0). This failure was consistent across model architectures, parameter scales (12B-272B), and conversational conditions. We posit that these findings may be driven by human imagination networks reflecting memory organization accumulated through embodied experience, a representational structure that linguistic training alone does not reproduce regardless of model scale and conversational memory."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04391","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:13:21Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:13:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-03T02:13:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03896","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03896","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03896","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingyu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Mingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyi","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Xiaoyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muzhi","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Muzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Canyu","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Canyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yating","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yating","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoyi","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Haoyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunhua","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Chunhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GAE: Unleashing Physical Potential of VLM with Generalizable Action Expert"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T18:33:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:40:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-11T03:17:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-12T00:23:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-language models demonstrate strong reasoning and planning abilities, yet grounding these predictions into precise robot actions remains a central challenge. Existing Vision-Language-Action methods typically entangle reasoning and action generation, leading to limited generalization. We propose Generalizable Action Expert (GAE), a task-agnostic model that converts sparse geometric plans into dense robot actions. Our approach introduces a sparse geometric interface: the VLM predicts sparse 3D waypoints representing high-level intention, while GAE maps these waypoints together with real-time point cloud observations to continuous action trajectories. GAE is pretrained on a large-scale pointcloud-trajectory dataset comprising 150k trajectories from both simulation and real-world robots. To further improve efficiency and generalization, we introduce an Action Pre-training, Pointcloud Fine-tuning (APPF) scheme that decouples learning action dynamics from geometry grounding. After pretraining, GAE is frozen and reused across downstream tasks, requiring only lightweight fine-tuning of the VLM to produce the sparse interface. Experiments show that our method achieves strong performance and generalization across diverse visual domains, camera viewpoints, and natural language instructions."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03896","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:01:11Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:01:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-12T02:19:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04567","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04567","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04567","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weishuo","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Weishuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanbo","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yanbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiyuan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muhan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Muhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GILT: An LLM-Free, Tuning-Free Graph Foundational Model for In-Context Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T08:09:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:19:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-22T05:06:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-25T00:24:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-10T08:33:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-11T00:39:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Graph Neural Networks (GNNs) are powerful tools for processing relational data but often struggle to generalize to unseen graphs, giving rise to the development of Graph Foundational Models (GFMs). However, current GFMs are challenged by the extreme heterogeneity of graph data, where each graph can possess a unique feature space, label set, and topology. To address this, two main paradigms have emerged. The first leverages Large Language Models (LLMs), but is fundamentally text-dependent, thus struggles to handle the numerical features in vast graphs. The second pre-trains a structure-based model, but the adaptation to new tasks typically requires a costly, per-graph tuning stage, creating a critical efficiency bottleneck. In this work, we move beyond these limitations and introduce \\textbf{G}raph \\textbf{I}n-context \\textbf{L}earning \\textbf{T}ransformer (GILT), a framework built on an LLM-free and tuning-free architecture. GILT introduces a novel token-based framework for in-context learning (ICL) on graphs, reframing classification tasks spanning node, edge and graph levels in a unified framework. This mechanism is the key to handling heterogeneity, as it is designed to operate on generic numerical features. Further, its ability to understand class semantics dynamically from the context enables tuning-free adaptation. Comprehensive experiments show that GILT achieves stronger few-shot performance with significantly less time than LLM-based or tuning-based baselines, validating the effectiveness of our approach. Our code is available at: https://github.com/yiming421/inductnode/."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted as an oral presentation at the GFM @ ICML 2026 Workshop"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04567","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:17:40Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:17:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-11T02:25:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04514","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04514","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04514","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rachneet","familyName":"Kaur","name":"Kaur, Rachneet","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nishan","familyName":"Srishankar","name":"Srishankar, Nishan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhen","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Zhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sumitra","familyName":"Ganesh","name":"Ganesh, Sumitra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manuela","familyName":"Veloso","name":"Veloso, Manuela","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ChartAgent: A Multimodal Agent for Visually Grounded Reasoning in Complex Chart Question Answering"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computational Engineering, Finance, and Science (cs.CE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T06:05:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:16:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-07T06:02:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-08T01:21:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-08T20:02:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-10T00:09:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent multimodal LLMs have shown promise in chart-based visual question answering, but their performance declines sharply on unannotated charts-those requiring precise visual interpretation rather than relying on textual shortcuts. To address this, we introduce ChartAgent, a novel agentic framework that explicitly performs visual reasoning directly within the chart's spatial domain. Unlike textual chain-of-thought reasoning, ChartAgent iteratively decomposes queries into visual subtasks and actively manipulates and interacts with chart images through specialized actions such as drawing annotations, cropping regions (e.g., segmenting pie slices, isolating bars), and localizing axes, using a library of chart-specific vision tools to fulfill each subtask. This iterative reasoning process closely mirrors human cognitive strategies for chart comprehension. ChartAgent achieves state-of-the-art accuracy on the ChartBench and ChartX benchmarks, surpassing prior methods by up to 16.07% absolute gain overall and 17.31% on unannotated, numerically intensive queries. Furthermore, our analyses show that ChartAgent is (a) effective across diverse chart types, (b) achieves the highest scores across varying visual and reasoning complexity levels, and (c) serves as a plug-and-play framework that boosts performance across diverse underlying LLMs. Our work is among the first to demonstrate visually grounded reasoning for chart understanding using tool-augmented multimodal agents."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at ACL 2026 (Main Conference). Also presented as an oral paper at the NeurIPS 2025 Multimodal Algorithmic Reasoning Workshop (https://marworkshop.github.io/neurips25/)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04514","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:16:22Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:16:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-10T02:30:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04195","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04195","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04195","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Puzhen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Puzhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuyang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xuyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhan","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Yuhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liqiu","familyName":"Meng","name":"Meng, Liqiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Constructing coherent spatial memory in LLM agents through graph rectification"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T13:27:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:57:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-08T23:11:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-10T00:15:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Given a map description through global traversal navigation instructions, an LLM can often infer the implicit spatial layout and answer user queries by providing shortest paths. However, such context-dependent querying becomes incapable as environments grow larger, motivating the need for incremental map construction that builds a complete topological graph from stepwise observations. We propose LLM-MapRepair, a framework for LLM-driven construction and map repair, designed to detect, localize, and correct structural inconsistencies in incrementally constructed navigation graphs. Our contributions include a Version Control mechanism for graph construction, an Edge Impact Score for repair prioritization, and a cleaned variant of the MANGO benchmark tailored for LLM-driven map construction and repair. We evaluate the framework on four evaluation settings: a synthetic per-component ablation (gpt-4.1, n=20 seeds per cell), a cross-vendor sweep over seven LLMs from OpenAI, Anthropic, and Google on both synthetic and TextWorld procedurally-generated text-adventure games, a repair-stage evaluation on all 42 cleaned-MANGO games with non-zero residual conflicts (534 conflicts; three vendors x three modes plus two non-LLM references), and an end-to-end natural-text deployment on Chapters 16-17 of Dream of the Red Chamber. On the DRC deployment, LLM-MapRepair achieves 94.3% node recall (+8.6 pp over direct LLM mapping) and 88.2% edge recall (+55.8 pp), using GPT-4.1; the recall improvements come with predicted node and edge counts that are roughly 4x the ground-truth counts (Table 4), reflecting the discretization-driven over-generation trade-off we discuss in the Limitations."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04195","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:08:33Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:08:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-10T02:30:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04704","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04704","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04704","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taoyuze","familyName":"Lv","name":"Lv, Taoyuze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexander","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Alexander","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fengyu","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Fengyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chu","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Chu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeffrey","familyName":"Meng","name":"Meng, Jeffrey","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongzhan","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Dongzhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingheng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yingheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bram","familyName":"Hoex","name":"Hoex, Bram","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhicheng","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Zhicheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tong","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Tong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AtomWorld: A Benchmark for Evaluating Spatial Reasoning in Large Language Models on Crystalline Materials"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Materials Science (cond-mat.mtrl-sci)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T11:17:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:27:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T04:08:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:23:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-22T05:18:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-23T01:20:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-28T12:47:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-05-29T01:02:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) have shown promising potential in scientific research, enabling tasks ranging from knowledge retrieval to property prediction. Existing science benchmarks mainly focus on perceptual or knowledge-based tasks, largely ignoring the modelling tasks, a fundamental starting point for any real scientific research. For materials science, constructing and manipulating atomic structures is one of the most creative and least automated steps. In this work, we introduce AtomWorld, a benchmark designed to evaluate the abilities of LLMs on structure modifications. The benchmark includes ten fundamental actions under four widely used modelling categories, enabling verifiable evaluation metrics. We find that Claude Opus 4.6 generally performs the best. While the success rate decreases markedly with increasing modelling complexity, with particularly low success rates (below 12\\% for rotation) for operations involving complex spatial relations. Our results suggest that contemporary LLMs are better suited as copilots for materials structure modelling rather than fully unsupervised autonomous scientific agents. Beyond evaluation, AtomWorld also serves as a testbed and playground for developing future structure-aware models, including reinforcement learning and agentic approaches."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04704","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:21:00Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:21:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-29T02:57:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03559","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03559","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03559","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeya","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zeya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianing","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Jianing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaxing","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Yaxing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Toby Jia-Jun","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Toby Jia-Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianshi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Tianshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PrivacyMotiv: Vulnerability-Centered Persona Journeys for Empathic Privacy Reviews in UX Design"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T23:14:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:14:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-20T18:22:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-22T00:04:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-26T20:35:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-28T00:12:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3800645.3813014","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"UX professionals routinely conduct design reviews, yet privacy concerns are often overlooked, not only due to limited tools, but more fundamentally from low intrinsic motivation, driven by limited privacy knowledge, weak empathy for unexpectedly affected users, and low autonomy in identifying harms. We present PrivacyMotiv, an LLM-powered system that generates vulnerability-centered personas, persona journey stories, and traceable design diagnoses grounded in lo-fi user flows to support privacy-oriented UX design review. In a within-subjects study with professional UX practitioners (N=16), PrivacyMotiv significantly improved empathy, intrinsic motivation, and perceived usefulness, with participants identifying 59% more privacy issues and proposing 70% more redesign solutions compared to self-proposed methods. This work contributes empirical insight into motivational barriers in privacy-aware UX and a structured, narrative-driven approach for integrating privacy review into early-stage UX practice."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"33 pages, 17 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03559","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-07T02:52:52Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:52:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-28T02:53:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03879","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03879","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03879","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Tianyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruishi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Ruishi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brandon","familyName":"Paulsen","name":"Paulsen, Brandon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Umang","familyName":"Mathur","name":"Mathur, Umang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Prateek","familyName":"Saxena","name":"Saxena, Prateek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Adversarial Agent Collaboration for Correctness Improvements of C to Safe Rust Translation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"D.2; I.2","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T17:08:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:39:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-08T09:32:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-09T02:22:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-17T11:10:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-19T01:07:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Translating C to memory-safe languages, like Rust, prevents critical memory safety vulnerabilities that are prevalent in legacy C software. Even with recent LLM-based and tool-augmented translators, the resulting Rust code frequently diverges from the C source on inputs absent from the test suite used during translation; this correctness gap on unseen inputs remains a dominant obstacle to reliable, automatic C-to-Rust translation. In this work, we present ACToR (Adversarial C To Rust), a simple LLM-agent loop that closes this gap by adversarially searching for inputs on which the translation diverges from the C source, and using them to drive subsequent refinements. Inspired by GANs, ACToR pits a translator agent against a discriminator agent that collaborate to iteratively refine the Rust translation. On each iteration, the translator agent synthesizes and refines a Rust translation to pass an existing suite of tests, and then the discriminator agent finds new failing tests by constructing and refining a differential fuzzer over the C and Rust binaries. Across 63 real-world command-line C utilities, with an average size of 473 lines of code and the longest reaching thousands of lines in size, ACToR achieves over 90% test pass rate with zero human intervention. The improvement holds across seven agent-LLM configurations on our micro-benchmark, indicating that the loop is largely independent of the choice of underlying translator and LLM. Compared to a non-adversarial, coverage-driven test-generation baseline, ACToR improves correctness by up to 36.7%. When applied on top of one recent translator, C2SaferRust, ACToR further improves the validation pass rate by 16.6%."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03879","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:00:45Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:00:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-19T04:48:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03992","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03992","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03992","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jehyeok","familyName":"Yeon","name":"Yeon, Jehyeok","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Isha","familyName":"Chaudhary","name":"Chaudhary, Isha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gagandeep","familyName":"Singh","name":"Singh, Gagandeep","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Quantitative Certification of Agentic Tool Selection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T01:50:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:46:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-13T14:28:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-14T01:11:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed in agentic systems, where a fundamental task is mapping user intents to relevant external tools. Errors in tool selection can have severe outcomes, such as unauthorized data access, even without modifying the agent's underlying model. Existing evaluations measure performance on curated, benign benchmarks. However, a pipeline's behavior in deployment depends on the tool pool the agent actually encounters, which in open registries is shaped by third parties. We introduce LLMCert-T, the first statistical framework that returns \\textbf{high-confidence upper bounds on the probability that a tool-selection pipeline satisfies a declared safety specification under a realistic tool distribution}. LLMCert-T models tool-selection evaluation as a Bernoulli estimation problem, drawing inserted-tool sequences from a distribution that the safety specification fixes. To evaluate robustness against realistic deployment conditions, we instantiate this distribution as a stochastic process that generates inserted-tool sequences round by round, conditioning each round on the agent's selection in the previous round. LLMCert-T aggregates the per-trial outcomes into a one-sided Clopper-Pearson upper bound on the probability that the specification is satisfied. By returning this bound as a certificate with statistical guarantees over the inserted-tool sequence distribution, LLMCert-T makes safety claims intuitive, actionable, and comparable across models, retrievers, mitigations, and registry policies. Across popular BFCL and OpenAPI tool pools, LLMCert-T shows that current LLM agents remain fragile under Distractor Selection and Top-N Saturation specifications: their certified correctness upper bounds drop to approximately 20\\%, far below their clean-pool lower bounds."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03992","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:03:33Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:03:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-14T02:50:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04166","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04166","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04166","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marco Edoardo","familyName":"Palma","name":"Palma, Marco Edoardo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pooja","familyName":"Rani","name":"Rani, Pooja","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harald C.","familyName":"Gall","name":"Gall, Harald C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multi Language Models for On-the-Fly Syntax Highlighting"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T11:48:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:55:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-04T18:14:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-06T00:03:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Syntax highlighting is a critical feature in modern software development environments, enhancing code readability and developer productivity. However, delivering accurate highlighting in real time remains challenging for online and web-based development tools due to strict time and memory constraints on backend services. These systems must serve highlights rapidly and frequently, even when code is partially valid or invalid. This has led to on-the-fly syntax highlighting, where visual annotations are generated just before content is served, often at high request rates and under incomplete input conditions. To meet these demands efficiently, state-of-the-art models use deep learning to learn the behavior of brute-force syntax highlighting resolvers, tools that are easy to implement but too slow for production. Through the Deep Abstraction process, brute-force strategies are encoded into fast statistical models that achieve both high accuracy and low-latency inference. Despite their success, such models face key challenges: they support only one programming language per model, require large datasets from slow brute-force generators, and involve resource-intensive training. In multi-language environments, this means maintaining multiple independent models, increasing system complexity and operational cost. This work addresses these issues by introducing a unified model capable of highlighting up to six mainstream programming languages, reducing deployment complexity by a factor of six and improving performance on unseen languages. A novel normalization technique significantly enhances model generalization, while few-shot learning experiments show that a small number of oracle samples can replace large datasets, minimizing dependence on brute-force generators. Combined, these innovations enable efficient, scalable, and cost-effective syntax highlighting across diverse programming languages."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04166","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:07:50Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:07:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-06T02:14:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04214","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04214","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04214","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xia","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Xia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yihan","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luhui","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Luhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ye","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Ye","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuoran","familyName":"Zhuang","name":"Zhuang, Zhuoran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Teaching LLM to be Persuasive: Reward-Enhanced Policy Optimization for Alignment from Heterogeneous Rewards"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T14:08:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:58:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-11T14:15:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T00:38:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-29T16:53:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-30T01:00:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We deploy large language models (LLMs) as business development (BD) agents for persuasive price negotiation in online travel agencies (OTAs). The agent must follow a multi-stage Standard Operating Procedure (SOP) and strict guardrails (no over-promising and no hallucinations), while remaining human-like and effective over long, multi-turn dialogues. We propose Reward-Enhanced Policy Optimization (REPO), a reinforcement learning post-training method that combines heterogeneous rewards: a preference-trained reward model (RM), an LLM-as-a-judge (RJ) for nuanced behaviors (e.g., emotional value and SOP compliance), and rule-based reward functions (RF) (mainly regex-based) for deterministic checks on numerics, formatting, and guardrails. In expert consensus evaluation (three human experts; 30 online conversations and 45 curated bad cases), REPO improves average dialogue rating to 4.63 (+0.33 over GRPO) and raises the share of conversations with at least one excellent response to 66.67% (+23.34 pp over GRPO), while achieving a 93.33% bad-case fix rate with 75.56% clean fixes. In a production A/B test on 9,653 real customer conversations (vs. an intent-driven dialogue system), REPO improves response rate by +12.14 pp and task success rate by +5.94 pp (p\u0026lt;0.001)."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"accepted by ACL 2026 indusry track"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04214","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:09:02Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:09:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-30T02:19:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04573","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04573","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04573","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoqiang","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Haoqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yizhe","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yizhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nikki Lijing","familyName":"Kuang","name":"Kuang, Nikki Lijing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicklas","familyName":"Majamaki","name":"Majamaki, Nicklas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Navdeep","familyName":"Jaitly","name":"Jaitly, Navdeep","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi-An","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Yi-An","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lianhui","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Lianhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LaDiR: Latent Diffusion Enhances LLMs for Text Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T08:15:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:19:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T17:58:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-08T01:03:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-13T07:01:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-14T01:35:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-11T22:01:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-12-15T01:09:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-03-03T09:27:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-03-04T01:38:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-04-23T01:00:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2026-04-24T00:15:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"6","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) demonstrate their reasoning ability through chain-of-thought (CoT) generation. However, LLM's autoregressive decoding may limit the ability to revisit and refine earlier tokens in a holistic manner, which can also lead to inefficient exploration for diverse solutions. In this paper, we propose LaDiR} (Latent Diffusion Reasoner), a novel reasoning framework that unifies the expressiveness of continuous latent representation with the iterative refinement capabilities of latent diffusion models for an existing LLM. We first construct a structured latent reasoning space using a Variational Autoencoder (VAE) that encodes text reasoning steps into blocks of thought tokens, preserving semantic information and interpretability while offering compact but expressive representations. Subsequently, we utilize a latent diffusion model that learns to denoise a block of latent thought tokens with a blockwise bidirectional attention mask, enabling longer horizon and iterative refinement with adaptive test-time compute. This design, combined with explicit diversity guidance during diffusion inference, enables the generation of multiple diverse reasoning trajectories that explore distinct regions of the latent space, rather than producing repetitive solutions as often occurs in standard autoregressive sampling. We conduct evaluations on a suite of mathematical reasoning, code generation and puzzle planning benchmarks. Empirical results show that LaDiR consistently improves accuracy, diversity, and interpretability over existing autoregressive, diffusion-based, and latent reasoning methods, revealing a new paradigm for text reasoning with latent diffusion."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04573","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":5,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:17:49Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:17:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-24T02:13:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04371","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04371","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04371","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Naimeng","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Naimeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arnav","familyName":"Ahuja","name":"Ahuja, Arnav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Georgios","familyName":"Liargkovas","name":"Liargkovas, Georgios","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunan","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Yunan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kostis","familyName":"Kaffes","name":"Kaffes, Kostis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyi","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Tianyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Speculative Actions: A Lossless Framework for Faster Agentic Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T21:28:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:07:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-23T17:58:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-24T01:04:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"AI agents are increasingly deployed in complex, interactive environments, yet their runtime remains a major bottleneck for training, evaluation, and real-world use. Typical agent behavior unfolds sequentially, with each action requiring an API call that can incur substantial latency. For example, a game of chess between two state-of-the-art agents can take hours. We introduce Speculative Actions, a lossless acceleration framework for general agentic systems. Inspired by speculative execution in microprocessors and speculative decoding in LLM inference, our method uses faster models to predict likely future actions and execute them in parallel, committing only when predictions match. We evaluate speculative actions across gaming, e-commerce, and web search environments, and additionally study a lossy extension in an operating systems setting. Across domains, we achieve up to 55% next-action prediction accuracy, translating into up to 20% latency reductions. Finally, we present a cost-latency analysis that formalizes the tradeoff between speculative breadth and time savings. This analysis enables principled tuning and selective branch launching to ensure that multi-branch speculation delivers practical speedups without prohibitive cost growth."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04371","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:12:52Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:12:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-24T02:13:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03323","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03323","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03323","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ge","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Ge","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinbo","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Jinbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiacheng","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jiacheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengfei","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Pengfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhao","familyName":"Shang","name":"Shang, Yuhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huiwen","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Huiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongli","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Hongli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanchun","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yuanchun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunxin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yunxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Enhancing Agentic Textual Graph Retrieval with Synthetic Stepwise Supervision"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T13:27:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:02:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-14T06:40:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-15T00:31:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-22T11:22:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-23T00:45:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Integrating textual graphs into Large Language Models (LLMs) is promising for complex graph-based QA. However, a key bottleneck is retrieving informative yet compact subgraphs that fit the LLM context. Existing retrievers often struggle, relying either on shallow embedding similarity or costly interactive policies that require excessive supervision. To address these challenges, we introduce an agentic textual graph reasoning framework featuring an LLM-based retriever trained with synthetic stepwise supervision. Rather than relying on final answer rewards which often yield sparse and unstable signals, we optimize the retriever by evaluating each step against offline-extracted golden subgraphs. Our approach distills golden subgraphs via a specialized data synthesis pipeline to formulate dense rewards, facilitating a two-stage training scheme that effectively learns the interactive graph exploration policy. Based on extensive experiments on three common datasets in comparison with seven strong baselines, our approach achieves an average improvement of 15.6% in accuracy and 17.2% in F1 score. The advantage is even higher in more complicated multi-hop reasoning tasks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03323","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:47:08Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:47:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-23T02:22:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03638","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03638","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03638","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jialin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jialin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lisang","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Lisang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stanley","familyName":"Osher","name":"Osher, Stanley","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wotao","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Wotao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Expressive Power of Implicit Models: Rich Equilibria and Test-Time Scaling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Representation Theory (math.RT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T02:49:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:19:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-28T21:40:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-02T01:08:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-01T02:46:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-03T01:57:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-31T02:07:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-04-01T00:19:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Implicit models, an emerging model class, compute outputs by iterating a single parameter block to a fixed point. This architecture realizes an infinite-depth, weight-tied network that trains with constant memory, significantly reducing memory needs for the same level of performance compared to explicit models. While it is empirically known that these compact models can often match or even exceed the accuracy of larger explicit networks by allocating more test-time compute, the underlying mechanism remains poorly understood.\n We study this gap through a nonparametric analysis of expressive power. We provide a strict mathematical characterization, showing that a simple and regular implicit operator can, through iteration, progressively express more complex mappings. We prove that for a broad class of implicit models, this process lets the model's expressive power scale with test-time compute, ultimately matching a much richer function class. The theory is validated across four domains: image reconstruction, scientific computing, operations research, and LLM reasoning, demonstrating that as test-time iterations increase, the complexity of the learned mapping rises, while the solution quality simultaneously improves and stabilizes."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03638","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:54:46Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:54:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-01T02:31:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04618","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04618","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04618","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qizheng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Qizheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changran","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Changran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shubhangi","familyName":"Upasani","name":"Upasani, Shubhangi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boyuan","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Boyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fenglu","familyName":"Hong","name":"Hong, Fenglu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vamsidhar","familyName":"Kamanuru","name":"Kamanuru, Vamsidhar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jay","familyName":"Rainton","name":"Rainton, Jay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengmeng","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Mengmeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanchen","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Hanchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Urmish","familyName":"Thakker","name":"Thakker, Urmish","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, James","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kunle","familyName":"Olukotun","name":"Olukotun, Kunle","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Agentic Context Engineering: Evolving Contexts for Self-Improving Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T09:30:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:22:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-29T19:22:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-02T01:03:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-29T09:18:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-31T00:55:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language model (LLM) applications such as agents and domain-specific reasoning increasingly rely on context adaptation: modifying inputs with instructions, strategies, or evidence, rather than weight updates. Prior approaches improve usability but often suffer from brevity bias, which drops domain insights for concise summaries, and from context collapse, where iterative rewriting erodes details over time. We introduce ACE (Agentic Context Engineering), a framework that treats contexts as evolving playbooks that accumulate, refine, and organize strategies through a modular process of generation, reflection, and curation. ACE prevents collapse with structured, incremental updates that preserve detailed knowledge and scale with long-context models. Across agent and domain-specific benchmarks, ACE optimizes contexts both offline (e.g., system prompts) and online (e.g., agent memory), consistently outperforming strong baselines: +10.6% on agents and +8.6% on finance, while significantly reducing adaptation latency and rollout cost. Notably, ACE could adapt effectively without labeled supervision and instead by leveraging natural execution feedback. On the AppWorld leaderboard, ACE matches the top-ranked production-level agent on the overall average and surpasses it on the harder test-challenge split, despite using a smaller open-source model. These results show that comprehensive, evolving contexts enable scalable, efficient, and self-improving LLM systems with low overhead."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026; 32 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04618","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":3,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:18:53Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:18:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-31T04:02:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04607","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04607","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04607","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingyu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Mingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haibo","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Haibo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Imperative to Declarative: Towards LLM-friendly OS Interfaces for Boosted Computer-Use Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Operating Systems (cs.OS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T09:14:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:21:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-25T11:29:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-26T00:47:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3767295.3803576","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Computer-use agents (CUAs) powered by large language models (LLMs) have emerged as a promising approach to automating computer tasks, yet they struggle with the existing human-oriented OS interfaces - graphical user interfaces (GUIs). GUIs force LLMs to decompose high-level goals into lengthy, error-prone sequences of fine-grained actions, resulting in low success rates and an excessive number of LLM calls.\n We propose Declarative Model Interface (DMI), an abstraction that transforms existing GUIs into three declarative primitives: access, state, and observation, thereby providing novel OS interfaces tailored for LLM agents. Our key idea is policy-mechanism separation: LLMs focus on high-level semantic planning (policy) while DMI handles low-level navigation and interaction (mechanism). DMI does not require modifying the application source code or relying on application programming interfaces (APIs).\n We evaluate DMI with Microsoft Office Suite (Word, PowerPoint, Excel) on Windows. Integrating DMI into a leading GUI-based agent baseline improves task success rates by 67% and reduces interaction steps by 43.5%. Notably, DMI completes over 61% of successful tasks with a single LLM call."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04607","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-07T03:18:37Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:18:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-26T02:29:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04468","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04468","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04468","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Asif Mohammed","familyName":"Samir","name":"Samir, Asif Mohammed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammad Masudur","familyName":"Rahman","name":"Rahman, Mohammad Masudur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Improving IR-based Bug Localization with Semantics-Driven Query Reduction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T03:43:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:13:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-17T23:49:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-19T00:29:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Despite decades of research, software bug localization remains challenging due to heterogeneous content and inherent ambiguities in bug reports. Existing methods, such as Information Retrieval (IR)-based approaches, often attempt to match source documents to bug reports, overlooking the context and semantics of the source code. On the other hand, Large Language Models (LLMs) (e.g., Transformer models) show promising results in understanding both texts and code. However, they have not yet been adapted well to localize software bugs using bug reports. They could also be data or resource-intensive. To bridge this gap, we propose, IQLoc, a novel approach that capitalizes on the strengths of both IR and LLMs for bug localization. In particular, we leverage the transformer-based model's understanding of code semantics to reason about its suspiciousness and to reformulate search queries and thus enhance bug localization using Information Retrieval. To evaluate IQLoc, we refine the Bench4BL benchmark dataset and extend it by incorporating ~30% more recent bug reports, resulting in a benchmark containing ~7.5K bug reports. We evaluated IQLoc using three performance metrics and compare it against eight baseline techniques. Experimental results demonstrate its superiority, achieving up to 100.40% and 78.08% in MAP, 61.49% and 64.58% in MRR, and 76.98% and 100.90% in HIT@K for the test bug reports with random and time-wise splits, respectively. Moreover, IQLoc improves MAP by 118.70% for bug reports with stack traces, 111.87% for those that include code elements, and 127.45% for those containing only descriptions in natural language. By integrating program semantic understanding into Information Retrieval, IQLoc mitigates several longstanding challenges of traditional IR-based approaches in bug localization."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"62 pages, 18 figures, 11 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04468","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:15:16Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:15:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-19T02:54:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04017","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04017","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04017","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sumanth","familyName":"Varambally","name":"Varambally, Sumanth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marshall","familyName":"Fisher","name":"Fisher, Marshall","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jas","familyName":"Thakker","name":"Thakker, Jas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiwei","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhirui","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Zhirui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yasaman","familyName":"Jafari","name":"Jafari, Yasaman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruijia","familyName":"Niu","name":"Niu, Ruijia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manas","familyName":"Jain","name":"Jain, Manas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Veeramakali Vignesh","familyName":"Manivannan","name":"Manivannan, Veeramakali Vignesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zachary","familyName":"Novack","name":"Novack, Zachary","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luyu","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Luyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Srikar","familyName":"Eranky","name":"Eranky, Srikar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Salva Rühling","familyName":"Cachay","name":"Cachay, Salva Rühling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taylor","familyName":"Berg-Kirkpatrick","name":"Berg-Kirkpatrick, Taylor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Duncan","familyName":"Watson-Parris","name":"Watson-Parris, Duncan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi-An","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Yi-An","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rose","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Rose","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Zephyrus: An Agentic Framework for Weather Science"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Atmospheric and Oceanic Physics (physics.ao-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T03:34:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:46:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-16T19:30:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-18T00:10:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Foundation models for weather science are pre-trained on vast amounts of structured numerical data and outperform traditional weather forecasting systems. However, these models lack language-based reasoning capabilities, limiting their utility in interactive scientific workflows. Large language models (LLMs) excel at understanding and generating text but cannot reason about high-dimensional meteorological datasets. We bridge this gap by building the first agentic framework for weather science. Our framework includes a Python code-based environment for agents (ZephyrusWorld) to interact with weather data, featuring tools including a WeatherBench 2 dataset indexer, geolocator for geocoding from natural language, weather forecasting, climate simulation capabilities, and a climatology module for querying precomputed climatological statistics (e.g., means, extremes, and quantiles) across multiple timescales. We design Zephyrus, a multi-turn LLM-based weather agent that iteratively analyzes weather datasets, observes results, and refines its approach through conversational feedback loops. We accompany the agent with a new benchmark, ZephyrusBench, with a scalable data generation pipeline that constructs diverse question-answer pairs across weather-related tasks, from basic lookups to advanced forecasting, extreme event detection, and counterfactual reasoning. Experiments on this benchmark demonstrate the strong performance of Zephyrus agents over text-only baselines, outperforming them by up to 44 percentage points in correctness. However, the hard tasks are still difficult even with frontier LLMs, highlighting the challenging nature of our benchmark and suggesting room for future development. Our codebase and benchmark are available at https://github.com/Rose-STL-Lab/Zephyrus."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04017","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:04:10Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:04:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-18T02:49:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03366","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03366","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03366","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harshwardhan","familyName":"Fartale","name":"Fartale, Harshwardhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ashish","familyName":"Kattamuri","name":"Kattamuri, Ashish","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rahul","familyName":"Raja","name":"Raja, Rahul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arpita","familyName":"Vats","name":"Vats, Arpita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ishita","familyName":"Prasad","name":"Prasad, Ishita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Akshata Kishore","familyName":"Moharir","name":"Moharir, Akshata Kishore","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Disentangling Recall and Reasoning in Transformer Models through Layer-wise Attention and Activation Analysis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T04:13:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:03:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-13T08:24:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-16T00:33:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Transformer-based language models excel at both recall (retrieving memorized facts) and reasoning (performing multi-step inference), but whether these abilities rely on distinct internal mechanisms remains unclear. Distinguishing recall from reasoning is crucial for predicting model generalization, designing targeted evaluations, and building safer interventions that affect one ability without disrupting the other.We approach this question through mechanistic interpretability, using controlled datasets of synthetic linguistic puzzles to probe transformer models at the layer, head, and neuron level. Our pipeline combines activation patching and structured ablations to causally measure component contributions to each task type. Across two model families (Qwen and LLaMA), we find that interventions on distinct layers and attention heads lead to selective impairments: disabling identified \"recall circuits\" reduces fact-retrieval accuracy by up to 15\\% while leaving reasoning intact, whereas disabling \"reasoning circuits\" reduces multi-step inference by a comparable margin. At the neuron level, we observe task-specific firing patterns, though these effects are less robust, consistent with neuronal polysemanticity.Our results provide the first causal evidence that recall and reasoning rely on separable but interacting circuits in transformer models. These findings advance mechanistic interpretability by linking circuit-level structure to functional specialization and demonstrate how controlled datasets and causal interventions can yield mechanistic insights into model cognition, informing safer deployment of large language models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03366","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:48:10Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:48:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-16T02:07:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04284","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04284","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04284","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunghwei","familyName":"Lai","name":"Lai, Yunghwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaiming","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Kaiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyue","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ziyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weizhi","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Weizhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Doctor-R1: Mastering Clinical Inquiry with Experiential Agentic Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T16:54:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:01:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-06T06:28:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-09T01:24:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-01T08:43:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-03T02:05:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The professionalism of a human doctor in outpatient service depends on two core abilities: the ability to make accurate medical decisions and the medical consultation skill to conduct strategic, empathetic patient inquiry. Existing Large Language Models (LLMs) have achieved remarkable accuracy on medical decision-making benchmarks. However, they often lack the ability to conduct the strategic and empathetic consultation, which is essential for real-world clinical scenarios. To address this gap, we propose Doctor-R1, an AI doctor agent trained to master both of the capabilities by ask high-yield questions and conduct strategic multi-turn inquiry to guide decision-making. Our framework introduces three key components: a multi-agent interactive environment, a two-tiered reward architecture that separately optimizes clinical decision-making and communicative inquiry skills, and an experience repository to ground policy learning in high-quality prior trajectories. We evaluate Doctor-R1 on OpenAI's HealthBench and MAQuE, assessed across multi-facet metrics, such as communication quality, user experience, and task accuracy. Remarkably, Doctor-R1 surpasses state-of-the-art open-source specialized LLMs by a substantial margin with higher parameter efficiency and outperforms powerful proprietary models. Furthermore, the human expert evaluations show that Doctor-R1 achieves superior clinical capability and patient-centric performance, demonstrating the effectiveness of the framework."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04284","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:10:44Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:10:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:44:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04067","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04067","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04067","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junxi","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Junxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zixi","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Zixi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingyao","familyName":"Ai","name":"Ai, Qingyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiqun","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yiqun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingtao","familyName":"Zhan","name":"Zhan, Jingtao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"What Scales in Cross-Entropy Scaling Law?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T07:06:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:50:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-28T08:15:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-03T01:33:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The cross-entropy scaling law has long served as a key tool for guiding the development of large language models. It shows that cross-entropy loss decreases in a predictable power-law rate as the model size increases. However, recent evidence indicates that this law breaks down at very large scales: the loss decreases more slowly than expected, which causes significant trouble for developing large language models. In this paper, we hypothesize that the root cause lies in the fact that cross-entropy itself does not truly scale; instead, only one of its hidden components does. To investigate this, we introduce a novel decomposition of cross-entropy into three parts: Error-Entropy, Self-Alignment, and Confidence. We show both theoretically and empirically that this decomposition precisely captures the training dynamics and optimization objectives. Through extensive experiments on multiple datasets and 32 models spanning five orders of magnitude in size, we find that only error-entropy follows a robust power-law scaling, while the other two terms remain largely invariant. Moreover, error-entropy constitutes the dominant share of cross-entropy in small models but diminishes in proportion as models grow larger. This explains why the cross-entropy scaling law appears accurate at small scales but fails at very large ones. Our findings establish the error-entropy scaling law as a more accurate description of model behavior. We believe it will have wide applications in the training, understanding, and future development of large language models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04067","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:05:22Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:05:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:44:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03253","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03253","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03253","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heyang","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Heyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zexu","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Zexu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Erxue","familyName":"Min","name":"Min, Erxue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hengyi","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Hengyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuaiqiang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shuaiqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dawei","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Dawei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xu","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Solving the Granularity Mismatch: Hierarchical Preference Learning for Long-Horizon LLM Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-26T08:43:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:00:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-02T07:26:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-03T02:46:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) as autonomous agents are increasingly tasked with solving complex, long-horizon problems. Aligning these agents via preference-based offline methods like Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) is a promising direction, yet it faces a critical granularity mismatch. Trajectory-level DPO provides a signal that is too coarse for precise credit assignment, while step-level DPO is often too myopic to capture the value of multi-step behaviors. To resolve this challenge, we introduce Hierarchical Preference Learning (HPL), a hierarchical framework that optimizes LLM agents by leveraging preference signals at multiple, synergistic granularities. While HPL incorporates trajectory- and step-level DPO for global and local policy stability, its core innovation lies in group-level preference optimization guided by a dual-layer curriculum. Our approach first decomposes expert trajectories into semantically coherent action groups and then generates contrasting suboptimal groups to enable preference learning at a fine-grained, sub-task level. Then, instead of treating all preference pairs equally, HPL introduces a curriculum scheduler that organizes the learning process from simple to complex. This curriculum is structured along two axes: the group length, representing sub-task complexity, and the sample difficulty, defined by the reward gap between preferred and dispreferred action groups. Experiments on three challenging agent benchmarks show that HPL outperforms existing state-of-the-art methods. Our analyses demonstrate that the hierarchical DPO loss effectively integrates preference signals across multiple granularities, while the dual-layer curriculum is crucial for enabling the agent to solve a wide range of tasks, from simple behaviors to complex multi-step sequences."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03253","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:45:24Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:45:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:44:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03495","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03495","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03495","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Erik","familyName":"Pautsch","name":"Pautsch, Erik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tanmay","familyName":"Singla","name":"Singla, Tanmay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Parv","familyName":"Kumar","name":"Kumar, Parv","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenxin","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Wenxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huiyun","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Huiyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Behnaz","familyName":"Hassanshahi","name":"Hassanshahi, Behnaz","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Konstantin","familyName":"Läufer","name":"Läufer, Konstantin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"George K.","familyName":"Thiruvathukal","name":"Thiruvathukal, George K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James C.","familyName":"Davis","name":"Davis, James C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AgentHub: A Registry for Discoverable, Verifiable, and Reproducible AI Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T20:18:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:09:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T18:01:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-27T02:00:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"LLM-based agents are rapidly proliferating, yet the infrastructure for discovering, evaluating, and governing them remains fragmented compared to mature ecosystems like software package registries (e.g., npm) and model hubs (e.g., Hugging Face). Existing efforts typically address naming, distribution, or protocol descriptors, but stop short of providing a registry layer that makes agents discoverable, comparable, and governable under automated reuse. We present AgentHub, a registry layer and accompanying research agenda for agent sharing that targets discovery and workflow integration, trust and security, openness and governance, ecosystem interoperability, lifecycle transparency, and capability clarity with evidence. We describe a reference prototype that implements a canonical manifest with publish-time validation, version-bound evidence records linked to auditable artifacts, and an append-only lifecycle event log whose states are respected by default in search and resolution. We also provide initial discovery results using an LLM-as-judge recommendation pipeline, showing how structured contracts and evidence improve intent-accurate retrieval beyond keyword-driven discovery. AgentHub aims to provide a common substrate for building reliable, reusable agent ecosystems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03495","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:51:18Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:51:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-27T03:15:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04373","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04373","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04373","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hadi","familyName":"Nekoei","name":"Nekoei, Hadi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aman","familyName":"Jaiswal","name":"Jaiswal, Aman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Patrice","familyName":"Bechard","name":"Bechard, Patrice","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oleh","familyName":"Shliazhko","name":"Shliazhko, Oleh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Orlando Marquez","familyName":"Ayala","name":"Ayala, Orlando Marquez","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mathieu","familyName":"Reymond","name":"Reymond, Mathieu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Massimo","familyName":"Caccia","name":"Caccia, Massimo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexandre","familyName":"Drouin","name":"Drouin, Alexandre","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sarath","familyName":"Chandar","name":"Chandar, Sarath","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexandre","familyName":"Lacoste","name":"Lacoste, Alexandre","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"JEF-Hinter: Leveraging Offline Knowledge for Improving Web Agents Adaptation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T21:34:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:07:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-20T19:34:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:04:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language model (LLM) agents perform well in sequential decision-making tasks, but improving them on unfamiliar domains often requires costly online interactions or fine-tuning on large expert datasets. These strategies are impractical for closed-source models and expensive for open-source ones, with risks of catastrophic forgetting. Offline trajectories offer reusable knowledge, yet demonstration-based methods struggle because raw traces are long, noisy, and tied to specific tasks. We present Just-in-time Episodic Feedback Hinter (JEF-Hinter), an agentic system that distills offline traces into compact, context-aware hints. A zooming mechanism highlights decisive steps in long trajectories, capturing both strategies and pitfalls. Unlike prior methods, JEF-Hinter leverages both successful and failed trajectories, extracting guidance even when only failure data is available, while supporting parallelized hint generation and benchmark-independent prompting. At inference, a retriever selects relevant hints for the current state, providing targeted guidance with transparency and traceability. Experiments on MiniWoB++, WorkArena-L1, and WebArena-Lite show that JEF-Hinter consistently outperforms strong baselines, including human- and document-based hints."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04373","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:12:55Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:12:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-24T04:00:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03346","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03346","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03346","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangyu","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Xiangyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marco","familyName":"Chiesa","name":"Chiesa, Marco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gerald Q.","familyName":"Maguire","name":"Maguire, Gerald Q.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dejan","familyName":"Kostic","name":"Kostic, Dejan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"KVComm: Enabling Efficient LLM Communication through Selective KV Sharing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T16:01:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:03:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-12T09:38:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-13T01:38:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-22T15:15:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:52:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed in multi-agent systems, where effective inter-model communication is crucial. Existing communication protocols either rely on natural language, incurring high inference costs and information loss, or on hidden states, which suffer from information concentration bias and inefficiency. To address these limitations, we propose KVComm, a novel communication framework that enables efficient communication between LLMs through selective sharing of KV pairs. KVComm leverages the rich information encoded in the KV pairs while avoiding the pitfalls of hidden states. We introduce a KV layer-wise selection strategy based on attention importance scores with a Gaussian prior to identify the most informative KV pairs for communication. Extensive experiments across diverse tasks and model pairs demonstrate that KVComm achieves comparable performance to the upper-bound method, which directly merges inputs to one model without any communication, while transmitting as few as 30\\% of layers' KV pairs. Our study highlights the potential of KV pairs as an effective medium for inter-LLM communication, paving the way for scalable and efficient multi-agent systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03346","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:47:41Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:47:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-24T04:00:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03669","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03669","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03669","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenlong","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Wenlong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yushu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yushu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boying","familyName":"Gong","name":"Gong, Boying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Danica J.","familyName":"Sutherland","name":"Sutherland, Danica J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoxiao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiaoxiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christos","familyName":"Thrampoulidis","name":"Thrampoulidis, Christos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Token Hidden Reward: Steering Exploration-Exploitation in Group Relative Deep Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T04:49:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-20T21:59:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-11T22:16:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-20T21:59:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-11T23:53:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-11-13T01:11:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-15T01:21:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-02-17T01:43:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards has significantly advanced the reasoning capabilities of large language models, yet how to explicitly steer training toward exploration or exploitation remains an open problem. We introduce Token Hidden Reward (THR), a token-level metric that quantifies each token's influence on the likelihood of correct responses under Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO). We find that training dynamics are dominated by a small subset of tokens with high absolute THR values. Most interestingly, tokens with positive THR strengthen confidence in correct outputs, thus favoring exploitation, while tokens with negative THR preserve probability mass for alternative outputs, enabling exploration. This insight suggests a natural intervention: a THR-guided reweighting algorithm that modulates GRPO's learning signals to explicitly bias training toward exploitation or exploration. We validate the efficacy of this algorithm on diverse math reasoning benchmarks. By amplifying tokens with positive THR value and weakening negative ones, our algorithm improves greedy-decoding accuracy, favoring exploitation. The reverse strategy yields consistent gains in Pass@K accuracy, favoring exploration. We further demonstrate that our algorithm integrates seamlessly with other RL objectives such as GSPO and generalizes across architectures including Llama. These findings establish THR as a principled and fine-grained mechanism for dynamically controlling exploration and exploitation in RL-tuned LLMs, providing new tools for targeted fine-tuning in reasoning-intensive applications."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Full version of submission to 2nd AI for Math Workshop@ ICML 2025 (best paper)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03669","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:55:31Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:55:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-17T04:46:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04787","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04787","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04787","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zifan","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Zifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaitao","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Kaitao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guosheng","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Guosheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ding","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Ding","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junyao","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Junyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaohua","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xiaohua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongsheng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Dongsheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cairong","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Cairong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Trade in Minutes! Rationality-Driven Agentic System for Quantitative Financial Trading"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T13:08:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:32:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-09T07:01:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-10T02:32:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advancements in large language models (LLMs) and agentic systems have shown exceptional decision-making capabilities, revealing significant potential for autonomic finance. Current financial trading agents predominantly simulate anthropomorphic roles that inadvertently introduce emotional biases and rely on peripheral information, while being constrained by the necessity for continuous inference during deployment. In this paper, we pioneer the harmonization of strategic depth in agents with the mechanical rationality essential for quantitative trading. Consequently, we present TiMi (Trade in Minutes), a rationality-driven multi-agent system that architecturally decouples strategy development from minute-level deployment. TiMi leverages specialized LLM capabilities of semantic analysis, code programming, and mathematical reasoning within a comprehensive policy-optimization-deployment chain. Specifically, we propose a two-tier analytical paradigm from macro patterns to micro customization, layered programming design for trading bot implementation, and closed-loop optimization driven by mathematical reflection. Extensive evaluations across 200+ trading pairs in stock and cryptocurrency markets empirically validate the efficacy of TiMi in stable profitability, action efficiency, and risk control under volatile market dynamics."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"17 pages, 6 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04787","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:23:04Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:23:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-10T05:21:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03944","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03944","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03944","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiqing","familyName":"He","name":"He, Weiqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianqi","familyName":"Shang","name":"Shang, Tianqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Li","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Li","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weijie","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Weijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Long","name":"Long, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On the Empirical Power of Goodness-of-Fit Tests in Watermark Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T21:07:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:43:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-05T21:10:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-09T01:07:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) raise concerns about content authenticity and integrity because they can generate human-like text at scale. Text watermarks, which embed detectable statistical signals into generated text, offer a provable way to verify content origin. Many detection methods rely on pivotal statistics that are i.i.d. under human-written text, making goodness-of-fit (GoF) tests a natural tool for watermark detection. However, GoF tests remain largely underexplored in this setting. In this paper, we systematically evaluate eight GoF tests across three popular watermarking schemes, using three open-source LLMs, two datasets, various generation temperatures, and multiple post-editing methods. We find that general GoF tests can improve both the detection power and robustness of watermark detectors. Notably, we observe that text repetition, common in low-temperature settings, gives GoF tests a unique advantage not exploited by existing methods. Our results highlight that classic GoF tests are a simple yet powerful and underused tool for watermark detection in LLMs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at NeurIPS 2025 as a spotlight"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03944","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:02:22Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:02:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-09T03:01:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03999","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03999","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03999","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuanming","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xuanming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samuel","familyName":"Yeh","name":"Yeh, Samuel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jwala","familyName":"Dhamala","name":"Dhamala, Jwala","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ousmane","familyName":"Dia","name":"Dia, Ousmane","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rahul","familyName":"Gupta","name":"Gupta, Rahul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sharon","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Sharon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LH-Deception: Simulating and Understanding LLM Deceptive Behaviors in Long-Horizon Interactions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T02:18:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:46:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-14T15:30:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-15T00:58:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-05T12:03:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-06T01:43:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Deception is a pervasive feature of human communication and an emerging concern in large language models (LLMs). While recent studies document instances of LLM deception, most evaluations remain confined to single-turn prompts and fail to capture the long-horizon interactions in which deceptive strategies typically unfold. We introduce a new simulation framework, LH-Deception, for a systematic, empirical quantification of deception in LLMs under extended sequences of interdependent tasks and dynamic contextual pressures. LH-Deception is designed as a multi-agent system: a performer agent tasked with completing tasks and a supervisor agent that evaluates progress, provides feedback, and maintains evolving states of trust. An independent deception auditor then reviews full trajectories to identify when and how deception occurs. We conduct extensive experiments across 11 frontier models, spanning both closed-source and open-source systems, and find that deception is model-dependent, increases with event pressure, and consistently erodes supervisor trust. Qualitative analyses further reveal emergent, long-horizon phenomena, such as ``chains of deception\", which are invisible to static, single-turn evaluations. Our findings provide a foundation for evaluating future LLMs in real-world, trust-sensitive contexts."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03999","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:03:42Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:03:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-06T03:21:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03267","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03267","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03267","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xianglong","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Xianglong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengzhu","familyName":"Bao","name":"Bao, Chengzhu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiteng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhiteng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Tianao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaicheng","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Kaicheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haotong","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Haotong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruobing","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Ruobing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingwu","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Xingwu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yulun","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yulun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PT$^2$-LLM: Post-Training Ternarization for Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-27T03:01:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:01:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-30T05:42:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-02T01:28:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have shown impressive capabilities across diverse tasks, but their large memory and compute demands hinder deployment. Ternarization has gained attention as a promising compression technique, delivering substantial size reduction and high computational efficiency. However, its potential in the post-training quantization (PTQ) setting remains underexplored, due to the challenge of training-free parameter optimization and the quantization difficulty posed by outliers and dispersed weights. To address these issues, we propose PT$^2$-LLM, a post-training ternarization framework tailored for LLMs. At its core is an Asymmetric Ternary Quantizer equipped with a two-stage refinement pipeline: (1) Iterative Ternary Fitting (ITF), which alternates between optimal ternary grid construction and flexible rounding to minimize quantization error, and (2) Activation-aware Grid Alignment (AGA), which further refines the ternary grid to better match full-precision outputs. In addition, we propose a plug-and-play Structural Similarity-based Reordering (SSR) strategy that leverages inter-column structural similarity to ease quantization and mitigate outlier effects, further enhancing overall performance. Extensive experiments demonstrate that PT$^2$-LLM delivers competitive performance against state-of-the-art (SOTA) 2-bit PTQ methods with lower memory cost, while also accelerating both prefill and decoding to achieve end-to-end speedup. The code and models will be available at https://github.com/XIANGLONGYAN/PT2-LLM."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03267","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:45:44Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:45:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-02T03:25:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03437","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03437","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03437","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jairo","familyName":"Diaz-Rodriguez","name":"Diaz-Rodriguez, Jairo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mumin","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Mumin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Consistent Kernel Change-Point Detection under m-Dependence for Text Segmentation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T18:57:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:06:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-26T18:36:37Z","dateType":"Withdrawn","dateInformation":"v2; This paper is withdrawn due to an error in the proof of Proposition 3, which is used to support Theorem 1"},{"date":"2026-01-27T02:46:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Kernel change-point detection (KCPD) has become a widely used tool for identifying structural changes in complex data. While existing theory establishes consistency under independence assumptions, real-world sequential data such as text exhibits strong dependencies. We establish new guarantees for KCPD under $m$-dependent data: specifically, we prove consistency in the number of detected change points and weak consistency in their locations under mild additional assumptions. We perform an LLM-based simulation that generates synthetic $m$-dependent text to validate the asymptotics. To complement these results, we present the first comprehensive empirical study of KCPD for text segmentation with modern embeddings. Across diverse text datasets, KCPD with text embeddings outperforms baselines in standard text segmentation metrics. We demonstrate through a case study on Taylor Swift's tweets that KCPD not only provides strong theoretical and simulated reliability but also practical effectiveness for text segmentation tasks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This paper is withdrawn due to an error in the proof of Proposition 3, which is used to support Theorem 1"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03437","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:49:53Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:49:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-27T04:35:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04633","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04633","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04633","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lukas","familyName":"Gienapp","name":"Gienapp, Lukas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martin","familyName":"Potthast","name":"Potthast, Martin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew","familyName":"Yates","name":"Yates, Andrew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harrisen","familyName":"Scells","name":"Scells, Harrisen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eugene","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Eugene","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Topic-Specific Classifiers are Better Relevance Judges than Prompted LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T09:38:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:22:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-23T14:11:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-26T01:39:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The unjudged document problem, where systems that did not contribute to the original judgement pool may retrieve documents without a relevance judgement, is a key obstacle to the reuseability of test collections in information retrieval. While the de facto standard to deal with the problem is to treat unjudged documents as non-relevant, many alternatives have been proposed, such as the use of large language models (LLMs) as a relevance judge (LLM-as-a-judge). However, this has been criticized, among other things, as circular, since the same LLM can be used as the ranker and the judge. We propose to train topic-specific relevance classifiers instead: By finetuning monoT5 with independent LoRA weight adaptation on the judgments of a single assessor for a single topic's pool, we align it to that assessor's notion of relevance for the topic. The system rankings obtained through our classifier's relevance judgments achieve a Spearmans' $ρ$ correlation of $\u0026gt;0.94$ with ground truth system rankings. As little as 128 initial human judgments per topic suffice to improve the comparability of models, compared to treating unjudged documents as non-relevant, while achieving more reliability than existing LLM-as-a-judge approaches. Topic-specific relevance classifiers are thus a lightweight and straightforward way to tackle the unjudged document problem, while maintaining human judgments as the gold standard for retrieval evaluation. Code, models, and data are made openly available."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 3 figures, 4 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04633","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:19:15Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:19:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-26T02:49:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03687","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03687","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03687","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanyuan","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yanyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dexuan","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Dexuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenzhuo","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Chenzhuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weihua","familyName":"Yue","name":"Yue, Weihua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MedReflect: Teaching Medical LLMs to Self-Improve via Reflective Correction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T06:00:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:24:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-16T10:35:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-19T01:29:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Medical problem-solving demands expert knowledge and intricate reasoning. Recent studies of large language models (LLMs) attempt to ease this complexity by introducing external knowledge verification through retrieval-augmented generation or by training on reasoning datasets. However, these approaches suffer from drawbacks such as retrieval overhead and high annotation costs, and they heavily rely on substituted external assistants to reach limited performance in medical field. In this paper, we introduce MedReflect, a generalizable framework designed to inspire LLMs with a physician-like reflective thinking mode. MedReflect generates a single-pass reflection chain that includes initial hypothesis generation, self-questioning, self-answering and decision refinement. This self-verified and self-reflective nature releases large language model's latent capability in medical problem-solving without external retrieval or heavy annotation. We demonstrate that MedReflect enables cost-efficient medical dataset construction. With only a minimal subset of randomly sampled training examples and lightweight fine-tuning, this approach achieves notable absolute accuracy improvements across a series of medical benchmarks while significantly cutting annotation requirements. Our results provide evidence that LLMs can learn to solve specialized medical problems via self-reflection and self-improvement, reducing reliance on external supervision and extensive task-specific fine-tuning data."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03687","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:55:57Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:55:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-19T02:45:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03405","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03405","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03405","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sanket","familyName":"Badhe","name":"Badhe, Sanket","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LegalSim: Multi-Agent Simulation of Legal Systems for Discovering Procedural Exploits"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T18:01:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-13T01:58:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.18653/v1/2025.nllp-1.27","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present LegalSim, a modular multi-agent simulation of adversarial legal proceedings that explores how AI systems can exploit procedural weaknesses in codified rules. Plaintiff and defendant agents choose from a constrained action space (for example, discovery requests, motions, meet-and-confer, sanctions) governed by a JSON rules engine, while a stochastic judge model with calibrated grant rates, cost allocations, and sanction tendencies resolves outcomes. We compare four policies: PPO, a contextual bandit with an LLM, a direct LLM policy, and a hand-crafted heuristic; Instead of optimizing binary case outcomes, agents are trained and evaluated using effective win rate and a composite exploit score that combines opponent-cost inflation, calendar pressure, settlement pressure at low merit, and a rule-compliance margin. Across configurable regimes (e.g., bankruptcy stays, inter partes review, tax procedures) and heterogeneous judges, we observe emergent ``exploit chains'', such as cost-inflating discovery sequences and calendar-pressure tactics that remain procedurally valid yet systemically harmful. Evaluation via cross-play and Bradley-Terry ratings shows, PPO wins more often, the bandit is the most consistently competitive across opponents, the LLM trails them, and the heuristic is weakest. The results are stable in judge settings, and the simulation reveals emergent exploit chains, motivating red-teaming of legal rule systems in addition to model-level testing."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages with 2 figures, accepted at the NLLP workshop at EMNLP 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03405","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-07T02:49:07Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:49:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-13T04:26:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03663","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03663","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03663","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangyu","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Xiangyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Can","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Can","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeyuan","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zeyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ran","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Ran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Caiming","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Caiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chien-Sheng","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Chien-Sheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"UNIDOC-BENCH: A Unified Benchmark for Document-Centric Multimodal RAG"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T04:30:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:19:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T05:30:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T00:30:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-05T04:16:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-06T01:58:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal retrieval-augmented Generation (MM-RAG) is a key approach for applying large language models (LLMs) and agents to real-world knowledge bases, yet current evaluations are fragmented -- focusing on either text or images in isolation, or simplified multimodal setup, failing to capture document-centric multimodal use cases. In this paper, we introduce UniDoc-Bench, the first large-scale, realistic benchmark for MM-RAG built from $k$ real-world PDF pages across domains. Our pipeline extracts and links evidence from text, tables, and figures, then generates multimodal QA pairs spanning factual retrieval, comparison, summarization, and logical reasoning queries. To ensure reliability, all of QA pairs are validated by multiple human annotators and expert adjudication. UniDoc-Bench supports apples-to-apples comparison across four paradigms: 1) text-only, 2) image-only, 3) \\emph{multimodal} text-image fusion and 4) multimodal joint retrieval -- under a unified protocol with standardized candidate pools, prompts, and evaluation metrics. UniDoc-Bench can also be used to evaluate Visual Question Answering (VQA) tasks. Our experiments show that multimodal text-image fusion RAG systems consistently outperform both unimodal and jointly multimodal embedding-based retrieval, indicating that neither text nor images alone are sufficient and that current multimodal embeddings remain inadequate. Beyond benchmarking, our analysis reveals when and how visual context complements textual evidence, uncovers systematic failure modes, and offers actionable guidance for developing more robust MM-RAG pipelines."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03663","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:55:22Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:55:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-06T04:10:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04564","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04564","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04564","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Honglin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Honglin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peng","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Peng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunfan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yunfan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xi","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Xi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Conditional Representation Learning for Customized Tasks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T08:00:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:19:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-13T08:39:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-16T01:26:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Conventional representation learning methods learn a universal representation that primarily captures dominant semantics, which may not always align with customized downstream tasks. For instance, in animal habitat analysis, researchers prioritize scene-related features, whereas universal embeddings emphasize categorical semantics, leading to suboptimal results. As a solution, existing approaches resort to supervised fine-tuning, which however incurs high computational and annotation costs. In this paper, we propose Conditional Representation Learning (CRL), aiming to extract representations tailored to arbitrary user-specified criteria. Specifically, we reveal that the semantics of a space are determined by its basis, thereby enabling a set of descriptive words to approximate the basis for a customized feature space. Building upon this insight, given a user-specified criterion, CRL first employs a large language model (LLM) to generate descriptive texts to construct the semantic basis, then projects the image representation into this conditional feature space leveraging a vision-language model (VLM). The conditional representation better captures semantics for the specific criterion, which could be utilized for multiple customized tasks. Extensive experiments on classification and retrieval tasks demonstrate the superiority and generality of the proposed CRL. The code is available at https://github.com/XLearning-SCU/2025-NeurIPS-CRL."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04564","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:17:35Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:17:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-16T04:50:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03291","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03291","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03291","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yizhuo","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Yizhuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wanying","familyName":"Qu","name":"Qu, Wanying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiawei","familyName":"Geng","name":"Geng, Jiawei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenqi","familyName":"Shao","name":"Shao, Wenqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanwei","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Yanwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"UniPruning: Unifying Local Metric and Global Feedback for Scalable Sparse LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-29T13:38:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:01:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-09T12:42:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-10T01:43:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) achieve strong performance across diverse tasks but face prohibitive computational and memory costs. Pruning offers a promising path by inducing sparsity while preserving architectural flexibility. However, existing methods struggle to balance efficiency and robustness: local metric approaches prune layer by layer but often collapse under high sparsity, whereas global feedback methods enforce consistency at the cost of expensive weight updates or restrictive semi-structured formats. We present UniPruning, a unified post-training pruning framework that combines the speed of local saliency metrics with the stability of global coordination, enabled by a mirror descent based optimization, all without updating model weights. UniPruning leverages fast layer-wise scoring and a lightweight global controller to allocate a single sparsity budget, supporting both unstructured and semi-structured N :M pruning within one framework. After a brief calibration, it can generate pruning masks for arbitrary sparsity levels in one shot, and adapts seamlessly to hardware-aware constraints. Extensive experiments on multiple pretrained LLM families and standard benchmarks show that UniPruning consistently delivers competitive or superior perplexity and zero-shot accuracy. Ablation studies further highlight the importance of mirror descent and local saliency anchoring. Overall, UniPruning provides an efficient, principled, and scalable solution for sparsifying large-scale LLMs. Our code is available at: https://github.com/RainbowQTT/UniPruning."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03291","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:46:21Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:46:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-10T03:16:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04002","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04002","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04002","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunkui","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yunkui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lanfei","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Lanfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianyu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jianyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nueraili","familyName":"Aierken","name":"Aierken, Nueraili","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Runhe","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Runhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongjian","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Hongjian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yibin","familyName":"Ying","name":"Ying, Yibin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shijian","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Shijian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AgriGPT-VL: Agricultural Vision-Language Understanding Suite"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T02:30:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:46:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T13:17:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:47:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-07T17:46:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-09T01:57:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Despite rapid advances in multimodal large language models, agricultural applications remain constrained by the scarcity of domain-tailored models, curated vision-language corpora, and rigorous evaluation. To address these challenges, we present the AgriGPT-VL Suite, a unified multimodal framework for agriculture. Our contributions are threefold. First, we introduce Agri-3M-VL, the largest vision-language corpus for agriculture to our knowledge, curated by a scalable multi-agent data generator; it comprises 1M image-caption pairs, 2M image-grounded VQA pairs, 50K expert-level VQA instances, and 15K GRPO reinforcement learning samples. Second, we develop AgriGPT-VL, an agriculture-specialized vision-language model trained via a progressive curriculum of textual grounding, multimodal shallow/deep alignment, and GRPO refinement. This method achieves strong multimodal reasoning while preserving text-only capability. Third, we establish AgriBench-VL-4K, a compact yet challenging evaluation suite with open-ended and image-grounded questions, paired with multi-metric evaluation and an LLM-as-a-judge framework. Experiments show that AgriGPT-VL outperforms leading general-purpose VLMs on AgriBench-VL-4K, achieving higher pairwise win rates in the LLM-as-a-judge evaluation. Meanwhile, it remains competitive on the text-only AgriBench-13K with no noticeable degradation of language ability. Ablation studies further confirm consistent gains from our alignment and GRPO refinement stages. We will open source all of the resources to support reproducible research and deployment in low-resource agricultural settings."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04002","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:03:47Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:03:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-09T04:37:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03480","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03480","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03480","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vali","familyName":"Tawosi","name":"Tawosi, Vali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Salwa","familyName":"Alamir","name":"Alamir, Salwa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaomo","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xiaomo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manuela","familyName":"Veloso","name":"Veloso, Manuela","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LLM Agents for Automated Dependency Upgrades"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T19:57:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:08:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-24T17:56:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-25T02:54:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As a codebase expands over time, its library dependencies can become outdated and require updates to maintain innovation and security. However, updating a library can introduce breaking changes in the code, necessitating significant developer time for maintenance. To address this, we introduce a framework of LLM agents to be used in combination with migration documentation to automatically recommend and apply code updates and ensure compatibility with new versions. Our solution can automatically localize updated library usages in live Java codebases and implement recommended fixes in a user-friendly manner. The system architecture consists of multiple key components: a Summary Agent, Control Agent, and Code Agent. To validate our approach, we apply the framework on an industrial use case by which we create three synthetic code repositories with major Upgrade changes and benchmark our approach against state-of-the-art methods. Results show that our approach not only performs upgrades using fewer tokens across all cases but also achieves a precision of 71.4%, highlighting its efficiency and effectiveness compared to state-of-the-art methods."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to AISM Workshop at ASE 2005"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03480","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:50:55Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:50:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-25T04:50:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03469","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03469","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03469","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keshav","familyName":"Ramani","name":"Ramani, Keshav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vali","familyName":"Tawosi","name":"Tawosi, Vali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Salwa","familyName":"Alamir","name":"Alamir, Salwa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Borrajo","name":"Borrajo, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Bridging LLM Planning Agents and Formal Methods: A Case Study in Plan Verification"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Logic in Computer Science (cs.LO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T19:46:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:08:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-24T18:17:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-25T02:55:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce a novel framework for evaluating the alignment between natural language plans and their expected behavior by converting them into Kripke structures and Linear Temporal Logic (LTL) using Large Language Models (LLMs) and performing model checking. We systematically evaluate this framework on a simplified version of the PlanBench plan verification dataset and report on metrics like Accuracy, Precision, Recall and F1 scores. Our experiments demonstrate that GPT-5 achieves excellent classification performance (F1 score of 96.3%) while almost always producing syntactically perfect formal representations that can act as guarantees. However, the synthesis of semantically perfect formal models remains an area for future exploration."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to AgenticSE Workshop at ASE 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03469","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:50:39Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:50:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-25T04:50:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03463","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03463","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03463","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vali","familyName":"Tawosi","name":"Tawosi, Vali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keshav","familyName":"Ramani","name":"Ramani, Keshav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Salwa","familyName":"Alamir","name":"Alamir, Salwa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaomo","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xiaomo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ALMAS: an Autonomous LLM-based Multi-Agent Software Engineering Framework"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T19:35:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:08:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-24T18:11:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-25T02:54:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multi-agent Large Language Model (LLM) systems have been leading the way in applied LLM research across a number of fields. One notable area is software development, where researchers have advanced the automation of code implementation, code testing, code maintenance, inter alia, using LLM agents. However, software development is a multifaceted environment that extends beyond just code. As such, a successful LLM system must factor in multiple stages of the software development life-cycle (SDLC). In this paper, we propose a vision for ALMAS, an Autonomous LLM-based Multi-Agent Software Engineering framework, which follows the above SDLC philosophy such that it may work within an agile software development team to perform several tasks end-to-end. ALMAS aligns its agents with agile roles, and can be used in a modular fashion to seamlessly integrate with human developers and their development environment. We showcase the progress towards ALMAS through our published works and a use case demonstrating the framework, where ALMAS is able to seamlessly generate an application and add a new feature."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to MAS-GAIN Workshop at ASE 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03463","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:50:30Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:50:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-25T04:50:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04173","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04173","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04173","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Soufiane","familyName":"Amini","name":"Amini, Soufiane","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yassine","familyName":"Benajiba","name":"Benajiba, Yassine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cesare","familyName":"Bernardis","name":"Bernardis, Cesare","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paul","familyName":"Cayet","name":"Cayet, Paul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hassan","familyName":"Chafi","name":"Chafi, Hassan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abderrahim","familyName":"Fathan","name":"Fathan, Abderrahim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Louis","familyName":"Faucon","name":"Faucon, Louis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Damien","familyName":"Hilloulin","name":"Hilloulin, Damien","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sungpack","familyName":"Hong","name":"Hong, Sungpack","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ingo","familyName":"Kossyk","name":"Kossyk, Ingo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tran Minh Son","familyName":"Le","name":"Le, Tran Minh Son","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rhicheek","familyName":"Patra","name":"Patra, Rhicheek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sujith","familyName":"Ravi","name":"Ravi, Sujith","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonas","familyName":"Schweizer","name":"Schweizer, Jonas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jyotika","familyName":"Singh","name":"Singh, Jyotika","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shailender","familyName":"Singh","name":"Singh, Shailender","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiyi","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Weiyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kartik","familyName":"Talamadupula","name":"Talamadupula, Kartik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jerry","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Jerry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Open Agent Specification (Agent Spec): A Unified Representation for AI Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T12:26:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:56:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T09:29:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:36:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-03T11:55:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-11-04T02:32:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-11-07T14:02:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-11-10T01:41:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The proliferation of agent frameworks has led to fragmentation in how agents are defined, executed, and evaluated. Existing systems differ in their abstractions, data flow semantics, and tool integrations, making it difficult to share or reproduce workflows. We introduce Open Agent Specification (Agent Spec), a declarative language that defines AI agents and agentic workflows in a way that is compatible across frameworks, promoting reusability, portability and interoperability of AI agents. Agent Spec defines a common set of components, control and data flow semantics, and schemas that allow an agent to be defined once and executed across different runtimes. Agent Spec also introduces a standardized Evaluation harness to assess agent behavior and agentic workflows across runtimes - analogous to how HELM and related harnesses standardized LLM evaluation - so that performance, robustness, and efficiency can be compared consistently across frameworks. We demonstrate this using four distinct runtimes (LangGraph, CrewAI, AutoGen, and WayFlow) evaluated over three different benchmarks (SimpleQA Verified, $τ^2$-Bench and BIRD-SQL). We provide accompanying toolsets: a Python SDK (PyAgentSpec), a reference runtime (WayFlow), and adapters for popular frameworks (e.g., LangGraph, AutoGen, CrewAI). Agent Spec bridges the gap between model-centric and agent-centric standardization \u0026amp; evaluation, laying the groundwork for reliable, reusable, and portable agentic systems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04173","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:08:01Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:08:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-10T02:49:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03490","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03490","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03490","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aneesha","familyName":"Sampath","name":"Sampath, Aneesha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oya","familyName":"Aran","name":"Aran, Oya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emily Mower","familyName":"Provost","name":"Provost, Emily Mower","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SEER: The Span-based Emotion Evidence Retrieval Benchmark"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T20:15:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:09:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:07:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-29T00:20:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce the SEER (Span-based Emotion Evidence Retrieval) Benchmark to test Large Language Models' (LLMs) ability to identify the specific spans of text that express emotion. Unlike traditional emotion recognition tasks that assign a single label to an entire sentence, SEER targets the underexplored task of emotion evidence detection: pinpointing which exact phrases convey emotion. This span-level approach is crucial for applications like empathetic dialogue and clinical support, which need to know how emotion is expressed, not just what the emotion is. SEER includes two tasks: identifying emotion evidence within a single sentence, and identifying evidence across a short passage of five consecutive sentences. It contains new annotations for both emotion and emotion evidence on 1200 real-world sentences. We evaluate 14 open-source LLMs and find that, while some models approach average human performance on single-sentence inputs, their accuracy degrades in longer passages. Our error analysis reveals key failure modes, including overreliance on emotion keywords and false positives in neutral text."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03490","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:51:11Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:51:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-29T02:23:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03369","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03369","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03369","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huazhen","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Huazhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huimin","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Huimin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hainbin","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Hainbin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lili","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Lili","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liangliang","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Liangliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenwen","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Wenwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TriQuest:An AI Copilot-Powered Platform for Interdisciplinary Curriculum Design"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T06:04:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-23T00:15:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-23T11:54:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-24T00:46:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Interdisciplinary teaching is a cornerstone of modern curriculum reform, but its implementation is hindered by challenges in knowledge integration and time-consuming lesson planning. Existing tools often lack the required pedagogical and domain-specific depth.We introduce TriQuest, an AI-copilot platform designed to solve these problems. TriQuest uses large language models and knowledge graphs via an intuitive GUI to help teachers efficiently generate high-quality interdisciplinary lesson plans. Its core features include intelligent knowledge integration from various disciplines and a human-computer collaborative review process to ensure quality and innovation.In a study with 43 teachers, TriQuest increased curriculum design efficiency and improved lesson plan quality. It also significantly lowered design barriers and cognitive load. Our work presents a new paradigm for empowering teacher professional development with intelligent technologies."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"16 pages, 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03369","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:48:14Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:48:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-24T02:15:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04774","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04774","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04774","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weixu","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Weixu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marco","familyName":"Dorigo","name":"Dorigo, Marco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mary Katherine","familyName":"Heinrich","name":"Heinrich, Mary Katherine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Online automatic code generation for robot swarms: LLMs and self-organizing hierarchy"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T12:49:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:31:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-19T16:00:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-21T00:55:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Our recently introduced self-organizing nervous system (SoNS) provides robot swarms with 1) ease of behavior design and 2) global estimation of the swarm configuration and its collective environment, facilitating the implementation of online automatic code generation for robot swarms. In a demonstration with 6 real robots and simulation trials with \u0026gt;30 robots, we show that when a SoNS-enhanced robot swarm gets stuck, it can automatically solicit and run code generated by an external LLM on the fly, completing its mission with an 85% success rate."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This abstract was accepted to and presented at the \"Multi-Agent Cooperative Systems and Swarm Robotics in the Era of Generative AI\" (MACRAI) workshop at the 2025 IEEE/RSJ International Conference on Intelligent Robots and Systems (IROS 2025)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04774","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:22:44Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:22:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-21T03:42:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04303","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04303","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04303","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Om","familyName":"Tailor","name":"Tailor, Om","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Audit the Whisper: Detecting Steganographic Collusion in Multi-Agent LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T17:51:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:03:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-17T19:12:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-21T00:07:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multi-agent deployments of large language models (LLMs) are increasingly embedded in market, allocation, and governance workflows, yet covert coordination among agents can silently erode trust and social welfare. Existing audits are dominated by heuristics that lack theoretical guarantees, struggle to transfer across tasks, and seldom ship with the infrastructure needed for independent replication. We introduce Audit the Whisper, a conference-grade research artifact that spans theory, benchmark design, detection, and reproducibility. Our contributions are: (i) a channel-capacity analysis showing how interventions such as paraphrase, rate limiting, and role permutation impose quantifiable capacity penalties-operationalised via paired-run Kullback--Leibler diagnostics-that tighten mutual-information thresholds with finite-sample guarantees and full proofs; (ii) ColludeBench-v0, covering pricing, first-price auctions, peer review, and hosted Gemini/Groq APIs with configurable covert schemes, deterministic manifests, and reward instrumentation; and (iii) a calibrated auditing pipeline that fuses cross-run mutual information, permutation invariance, watermark variance, and fairness-aware acceptance bias, each tuned to a $10^{-3}$ false-positive budget and validated by 10k honest runs plus an e-value martingale. Across ColludeBench and external suites including Secret Collusion, CASE, Perfect Collusion Benchmark, and SentinelAgent, the union meta-test attains state-of-the-art power at fixed FPR while ablations surface price-of-auditing trade-offs and fairness-driven colluders invisible to MI alone. We release regeneration scripts, anonymized manifests, and documentation so that external auditors can reproduce every figure, satisfy double-blind requirements, and extend the framework with minimal effort."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages, 0 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04303","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:11:12Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:11:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-21T03:42:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04587","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04587","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04587","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sheng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Sheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruiming","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Ruiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Charles","familyName":"Herndon","name":"Herndon, Charles","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yihang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yihang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shunsuke","familyName":"Koga","name":"Koga, Shunsuke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeanne","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Jeanne","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhi","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Zhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Pathology-CoT: Learning Visual Chain-of-Thought Agent from Expert Whole Slide Image Diagnosis Behavior"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T08:44:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:20:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-13T18:37:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-15T00:03:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diagnosing a whole-slide image is an interactive, multi-stage process of changing magnification and moving between fields. Although recent pathology foundation models demonstrated superior performances, practical agentic systems that decide what field to examine next, adjust magnification, and deliver explainable diagnoses are still lacking. Such limitation is largely bottlenecked by data: scalable, clinically aligned supervision of expert viewing behavior that is tacit and experience-based, not documented in textbooks or internet, and therefore absent from LLM training. Here we introduce a framework designed to address this challenge through three key breakthroughs. First, the AI Session Recorder seamlessly integrates with standard whole-slide image viewers to unobtrusively record routine navigation and convert the viewer logs into standardized behavioral commands and bounding boxes. Second, a lightweight human-in-the-loop review turns AI-drafted rationales for behavioral commands into the Pathology-CoT dataset, a form of paired \"where to look\" and \"why it matters\", enabling six-fold faster labeling compared to manual constructing such Chain-of-Thought dataset. Using this behavioral data, we build Pathology-o3, a two-stage agent that first proposes important ROIs and then performs behavior-guided reasoning. On the gastrointestinal lymph-node metastasis detection task, our method achieved 100 recall on the internal validation from Stanford Medicine and 97.6 recall on an independent external validation from Sweden, exceeding the state-of-the-art OpenAI o3 model and generalizing across backbones. To our knowledge, Pathology-CoT constitutes one of the first behavior-grounded agentic systems in pathology. Turning everyday viewer logs into scalable, expert-validated supervision, our framework makes agentic pathology practical and establishes a path to human-aligned, upgradeable clinical AI."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04587","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:18:08Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:18:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-15T02:22:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04550","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04550","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04550","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengfei","familyName":"He","name":"He, Pengfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenwei","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Zhenwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bing","familyName":"He","name":"He, Bing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hui","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Hui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xianfeng","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Xianfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanqing","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Hanqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juanhui","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Juanhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiayuan","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Jiayuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Subhabrata","familyName":"Mukherjee","name":"Mukherjee, Subhabrata","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Suhang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Suhang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Xing","name":"Xing, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiliang","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Jiliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benoit","familyName":"Dumoulin","name":"Dumoulin, Benoit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TRAJECT-Bench:A Trajectory-Aware Benchmark for Evaluating Agentic Tool Use"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T07:30:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:18:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-11T09:19:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T00:31:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language model (LLM)-based agents increasingly rely on tool use to complete real-world tasks. While existing works evaluate the LLMs' tool use capability, they largely focus on the final answers yet overlook the detailed tool usage trajectory, i.e., whether tools are selected, parameterized, and ordered correctly. We introduce TRAJECT-Bench, a trajectory-aware benchmark to comprehensively evaluate LLMs' tool use capability through diverse tasks with fine-grained evaluation metrics. TRAJECT-Bench pairs high-fidelity, executable tools across practical domains with tasks grounded in production-style APIs, and synthesizes trajectories that vary in breadth (parallel calls) and depth (interdependent chains). Besides final accuracy, TRAJECT-Bench also reports trajectory-level diagnostics, including tool selection and argument correctness, and dependency/order satisfaction. Analyses reveal failure modes such as similar tool confusion and parameter-blind selection, and scaling behavior with tool diversity and trajectory length where the bottleneck of transiting from short to mid-length trajectories is revealed, offering actionable guidance for LLMs' tool use."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04550","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:17:15Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:17:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-14T04:23:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03418","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03418","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03418","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ananya","familyName":"Mantravadi","name":"Mantravadi, Ananya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shivali","familyName":"Dalmia","name":"Dalmia, Shivali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Olga","familyName":"Pospelova","name":"Pospelova, Olga","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abhishek","familyName":"Mukherji","name":"Mukherji, Abhishek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nand","familyName":"Dave","name":"Dave, Nand","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anudha","familyName":"Mittal","name":"Mittal, Anudha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LegalWiz: A Multi-Agent Generation Framework for Contradiction Detection in Legal Documents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T18:24:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:05:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-10T19:46:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T00:07:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) integrates large language models (LLMs) with external sources, but unresolved contradictions in retrieved evidence often lead to hallucinations and legally unsound outputs. Benchmarks currently used for contradiction detection lack domain realism, cover only limited conflict types, and rarely extend beyond single-sentence pairs, making them unsuitable for legal applications. Controlled generation of documents with embedded contradictions is therefore essential: it enables systematic stress-testing of models, ensures coverage of diverse conflict categories, and provides a reliable basis for evaluating contradiction detection and resolution. We present a multi-agent contradiction-aware benchmark framework for the legal domain that generates synthetic legal-style documents, injects six structured contradiction types, and models both self- and pairwise inconsistencies. Automated contradiction mining is combined with human-in-the-loop validation to guarantee plausibility and fidelity. This benchmark offers one of the first structured resources for contradiction-aware evaluation in legal RAG pipelines, supporting more consistent, interpretable, and trustworthy systems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03418","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:49:26Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:49:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-14T04:23:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03683","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03683","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03683","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nisar","familyName":"Hussain","name":"Hussain, Nisar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amna","familyName":"Qasim","name":"Qasim, Amna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gull","familyName":"Mehak","name":"Mehak, Gull","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muhammad","familyName":"Zain","name":"Zain, Muhammad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Momina","familyName":"Hafeez","name":"Hafeez, Momina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Grigori","familyName":"Sidorov","name":"Sidorov, Grigori","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Fine-Tuning Large Language Models with QLoRA for Offensive Language Detection in Roman Urdu-English Code-Mixed Text"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T05:38:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:24:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-10T05:42:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:24:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The use of derogatory terms in languages that employ code mixing, such as Roman Urdu, presents challenges for Natural Language Processing systems due to unstated grammar, inconsistent spelling, and a scarcity of labeled data. In this work, we propose a QLoRA based fine tuning framework to improve offensive language detection in Roman Urdu-English text. We translated the Roman Urdu-English code mixed dataset into English using Google Translate to leverage English LLMs, while acknowledging that this translation reduces direct engagement with code mixing features. Our focus is on classification performance using English translated low resource inputs. We fine tuned several transformers and large language models, including Meta LLaMA 3 8B, Mistral 7B v0.1, LLaMA 2 7B, ModernBERT, and RoBERTa, with QLoRA for memory efficient adaptation. Models were trained and evaluated on a manually annotated Roman Urdu dataset for offensive vs non offensive content. Of all tested models, the highest F1 score of 91.45 was attained by Meta LLaMA 3 8B, followed by Mistral 7B at 89.66, surpassing traditional transformer baselines. These results demonstrate the efficacy of QLoRA in fine tuning high performing models for low resource environments such as code mixed offensive language detection, and confirm the potential of LLMs for this task. This work advances a scalable approach to Roman Urdu moderation and paves the way for future multilingual offensive detection systems based on LLMs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"25 pages, 22 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03683","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:55:51Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:55:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-13T02:17:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03298","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03298","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03298","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongqi","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Dongqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenjin","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Wenjin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CAFL-L: Constraint-Aware Federated Learning with Lagrangian Dual Optimization for On-Device Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-29T22:07:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:01:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-10T15:06:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:55:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce Constraint-Aware Federated Learning with Lagrangian Dual Optimization (CAFL-L), a principled extension of FedAvg that explicitly incorporates device-level resource constraints including energy, communication, memory, and thermal budgets. CAFL-L employs Lagrangian dual optimization to dynamically adapt training hyperparameters -- freezing depth, local steps, batch size, and communication compression -- while preserving training stability through token-budget preservation via gradient accumulation. Experiments on a character-level language model demonstrate that CAFL-L achieves superior constraint satisfaction compared to standard FedAvg (reducing memory usage by 20% and communication by 95%) while maintaining competitive validation performance, making it practical for deployment on resource-constrained edge devices."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by 39th NeurIPS - Constrained Optimization for Machine Learning"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03298","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:46:32Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:46:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-13T02:17:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04097","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04097","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04097","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peichao","familyName":"Lai","name":"Lai, Peichao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinhui","familyName":"Zhuang","name":"Zhuang, Jinhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kexuan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Kexuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ningchang","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Ningchang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengjie","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shengjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanwei","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Yanwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Chong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yilei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yilei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"WebRenderBench: Enhancing Web Interface Generation through Layout-Style Consistency and Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T08:47:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:52:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T03:04:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T00:24:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Automating the conversion of UI images into web code is a critical task for front-end development and rapid prototyping. Advances in multimodal large language models (MLLMs) have made WebUI-to-Code increasingly feasible, yet existing benchmarks remain limited in data diversity and evaluation reliability. To address these issues, we present WebRenderBench, a large-scale benchmark of 45.1k webpages collected from real-world portal sites, offering greater diversity, complexity, and realism than prior benchmarks. We further propose a novel evaluation metric that measures layout and style consistency from the final rendered pages. Unlike vision-based methods that rely on costly LLM reasoning or structure-based comparisons vulnerable to noise and asymmetry, our approach enables more efficient, objective, and reliable UI quality assessment. Finally, we introduce the Automated Layout and Style Inspection Agent (ALISA), which integrates this metric into reinforcement learning as a reward signal to enhance training on crawled asymmetric webpages. Experiments show that ALISA significantly boosts generation performance, achieving state-of-the-art results across multiple metrics."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04097","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:06:09Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:06:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-10T02:39:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04631","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04631","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04631","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anastasia","familyName":"Zhukova","name":"Zhukova, Anastasia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonas","familyName":"Lührs","name":"Lührs, Jonas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christian E.","familyName":"Lobmüller","name":"Lobmüller, Christian E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bela","familyName":"Gipp","name":"Gipp, Bela","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Contrastive Learning Using Graph Embeddings for Domain Adaptation of Language Models in the Process Industry"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T09:36:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:22:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T12:23:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:44:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent trends in NLP utilize knowledge graphs (KGs) to enhance pretrained language models by incorporating additional knowledge from the graph structures to learn domain-specific terminology or relationships between documents that might otherwise be overlooked. This paper explores how SciNCL, a graph-aware neighborhood contrastive learning methodology originally designed for scientific publications, can be applied to the process industry domain, where text logs contain crucial information about daily operations and are often structured as sparse KGs. Our experiments demonstrate that language models fine-tuned with triplets derived from graph embeddings (GE) outperform a state-of-the-art mE5-large text encoder by 9.8-14.3% (5.45-7.96p) on the proprietary process industry text embedding benchmark (PITEB) while having 3 times fewer parameters."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"accepted to EMNLP 2025 (industry track)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04631","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:19:12Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:19:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:27:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04796","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04796","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04796","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samah","familyName":"Kansab","name":"Kansab, Samah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Francis","familyName":"Bordeleau","name":"Bordeleau, Francis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ali","familyName":"Tizghadam","name":"Tizghadam, Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RevMine: An LLM-Assisted Tool for Code Review Mining and Analysis Across Git Platforms"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T13:22:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:33:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal","rightsIdentifier":"cc0-1.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Empirical research on code review processes is increasingly central to understanding software quality and collaboration. However, collecting and analyzing review data remains a time-consuming and technically intensive task. Most researchers follow similar workflows - writing ad hoc scripts to extract, filter, and analyze review data from platforms like GitHub and GitLab. This paper introduces RevMine, a conceptual tool that streamlines the entire code review mining pipeline using large language models (LLMs). RevMine guides users through authentication, endpoint discovery, and natural language-driven data collection, significantly reducing the need for manual scripting. After retrieving review data, it supports both quantitative and qualitative analysis based on user-defined filters or LLM-inferred patterns. This poster outlines the tool's architecture, use cases, and research potential. By lowering the barrier to entry, RevMine aims to democratize code review mining and enable a broader range of empirical software engineering studies."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04796","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:23:18Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:23:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:23:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04791","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04791","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04791","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kristian","familyName":"Kolthoff","name":"Kolthoff, Kristian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Felix","familyName":"Kretzer","name":"Kretzer, Felix","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simone Paolo","familyName":"Ponzetto","name":"Ponzetto, Simone Paolo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexander","familyName":"Maedche","name":"Maedche, Alexander","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christian","familyName":"Bartelt","name":"Bartelt, Christian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GUISpector: An MLLM Agent Framework for Automated Verification of Natural Language Requirements in GUI Prototypes"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T13:15:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:32:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"GUIs are foundational to interactive systems and play a pivotal role in early requirements elicitation through prototyping. Ensuring that GUI implementations fulfill NL requirements is essential for robust software engineering, especially as LLM-driven programming agents become increasingly integrated into development workflows. Existing GUI testing approaches, whether traditional or LLM-driven, often fall short in handling the complexity of modern interfaces, and typically lack actionable feedback and effective integration with automated development agents. In this paper, we introduce GUISpector, a novel framework that leverages a multi-modal (M)LLM-based agent for the automated verification of NL requirements in GUI prototypes. First, GUISpector adapts a MLLM agent to interpret and operationalize NL requirements, enabling to autonomously plan and execute verification trajectories across GUI applications. Second, GUISpector systematically extracts detailed NL feedback from the agent's verification process, providing developers with actionable insights that can be used to iteratively refine the GUI artifact or directly inform LLM-based code generation in a closed feedback loop. Third, we present an integrated tool that unifies these capabilities, offering practitioners an accessible interface for supervising verification runs, inspecting agent rationales and managing the end-to-end requirements verification process. We evaluated GUISpector on a comprehensive set of 150 requirements based on 900 acceptance criteria annotations across diverse GUI applications, demonstrating effective detection of requirement satisfaction and violations and highlighting its potential for seamless integration of actionable feedback into automated LLM-driven development workflows. The video presentation of GUISpector is available at: https://youtu.be/JByYF6BNQeE, showcasing its main capabilities."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04791","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:23:11Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:23:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:23:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04757","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04757","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04757","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eduardo Martínez","familyName":"Rivera","name":"Rivera, Eduardo Martínez","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Filippo","familyName":"Menolascina","name":"Menolascina, Filippo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ModernBERT + ColBERT: Enhancing biomedical RAG through an advanced re-ranking retriever"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Quantitative Methods (q-bio.QM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T12:34:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:30:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) is a powerful technique for enriching Large Language Models (LLMs) with external knowledge, allowing for factually grounded responses, a critical requirement in high-stakes domains such as healthcare. However, the efficacy of RAG systems is fundamentally restricted by the performance of their retrieval module, since irrelevant or semantically misaligned documents directly compromise the accuracy of the final generated response. General-purpose dense retrievers can struggle with the nuanced language of specialised domains, while the high accuracy of in-domain models is often achieved at prohibitive computational costs. In this work, we aim to address this trade-off by developing and evaluating a two-stage retrieval architecture that combines a lightweight ModernBERT bidirectional encoder for efficient initial candidate retrieval with a ColBERTv2 late-interaction model for fine-grained re-ranking. We conduct comprehensive evaluations of our retriever module performance and RAG system performance in the biomedical context, fine-tuning the IR module using 10k question-passage pairs from PubMedQA. Our analysis of the retriever module confirmed the positive impact of the ColBERT re-ranker, which improved Recall@3 by up to 4.2 percentage points compared to its retrieve-only counterpart. When integrated into the biomedical RAG, our IR module leads to a state-of-the-art average accuracy of 0.4448 on the five tasks of the MIRAGE question-answering benchmark, outperforming strong baselines such as MedCPT (0.4436). Our ablation studies reveal that this performance is critically dependent on a joint fine-tuning process that aligns the retriever and re-ranker; otherwise, the re-ranker might degrade the performance."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04757","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:22:19Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:22:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:22:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04749","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04749","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04749","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samy","familyName":"Ateia","name":"Ateia, Samy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Udo","familyName":"Kruschwitz","name":"Kruschwitz, Udo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Melanie","familyName":"Scholz","name":"Scholz, Melanie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Agnes","familyName":"Koschmider","name":"Koschmider, Agnes","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Moayad","familyName":"Almohaishi","name":"Almohaishi, Moayad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LLM-Based Information Extraction to Support Scientific Literature Research and Publication Workflows"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Digital Libraries (cs.DL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T12:27:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:30:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1007/978-3-032-06136-2_9","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The increasing volume of scholarly publications requires advanced tools for efficient knowledge discovery and management. This paper introduces ongoing work on a system using Large Language Models (LLMs) for the semantic extraction of key concepts from scientific documents. Our research, conducted within the German National Research Data Infrastructure for and with Computer Science (NFDIxCS) project, seeks to support FAIR (Findable, Accessible, Interoperable, and Reusable) principles in scientific publishing. We outline our explorative work, which uses in-context learning with various LLMs to extract concepts from papers, initially focusing on the Business Process Management (BPM) domain. A key advantage of this approach is its potential for rapid domain adaptation, often requiring few or even zero examples to define extraction targets for new scientific fields. We conducted technical evaluations to compare the performance of commercial and open-source LLMs and created an online demo application to collect feedback from an initial user-study. Additionally, we gathered insights from the computer science research community through user stories collected during a dedicated workshop, actively guiding the ongoing development of our future services. These services aim to support structured literature reviews, concept-based information retrieval, and integration of extracted knowledge into existing knowledge graphs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This PDF is the author-prepared camera-ready version corresponding to the accepted manuscript and supersedes the submitted version that was inadvertently published as the version of record"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04749","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-07T03:22:07Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:22:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:22:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04695","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04695","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04695","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiding","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yiding","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhepei","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Zhepei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyu","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Xinyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Meng","name":"Meng, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Beyond Outcome Reward: Decoupling Search and Answering Improves LLM Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T11:09:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:27:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Enabling large language models (LLMs) to utilize search tools offers a promising path to overcoming fundamental limitations such as knowledge cutoffs and hallucinations. Recent work has explored reinforcement learning (RL) for training search-augmented agents that interleave reasoning and retrieval before answering. These approaches usually rely on outcome-based rewards (e.g., exact match), implicitly assuming that optimizing for final answers will also yield effective intermediate search behaviors. Our analysis challenges this assumption: we uncover multiple systematic deficiencies in search that arise under outcome-only training and ultimately degrade final answer quality, including failure to invoke tools, invalid queries, and redundant searches. To address these shortcomings, we introduce DeSA (Decoupling Search-and-Answering), a simple two-stage training framework that explicitly separates search optimization from answer generation. In Stage 1, agents are trained to improve search effectiveness with retrieval recall-based rewards. In Stage 2, outcome rewards are employed to optimize final answer generation. Across seven QA benchmarks, DeSA-trained agents consistently improve search behaviors, delivering substantially higher search recall and answer accuracy than outcome-only baselines. Notably, DeSA outperforms single-stage training approaches that simultaneously optimize recall and outcome rewards, underscoring the necessity of explicitly decoupling the two objectives."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04695","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:20:45Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:20:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:20:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04678","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04678","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04678","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhanfeng","familyName":"Mo","name":"Mo, Zhanfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingxuan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xingxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuntao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yuntao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lidong","familyName":"Bing","name":"Bing, Lidong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multi-Agent Tool-Integrated Policy Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T10:44:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:26:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) increasingly rely on multi-turn tool-integrated planning for knowledge-intensive and complex reasoning tasks. Existing implementations typically rely on a single agent, but they suffer from limited context length and noisy tool responses. A natural solution is to adopt a multi-agent framework with planner- and worker-agents to manage context. However, no existing methods support effective reinforcement learning post-training of tool-integrated multi-agent frameworks. To address this gap, we propose Multi-Agent Tool-Integrated Policy Optimization (MATPO), which enables distinct roles (planner and worker) to be trained within a single LLM instance using role-specific prompts via reinforcement learning. MATPO is derived from a principled credit assignment mechanism across planner and worker rollouts. This design eliminates the need to deploy multiple LLMs, which would be memory-intensive, while preserving the benefits of specialization. Experiments on GAIA-text, WebWalkerQA, and FRAMES show that MATPO consistently outperforms single-agent baselines by an average of 18.38% relative improvement in performance and exhibits greater robustness to noisy tool outputs. Our findings highlight the effectiveness of unifying multiple agent roles within a single LLM and provide practical insights for stable and efficient multi-agent RL training."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Work in progress"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04678","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:20:21Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:20:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:20:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04648","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04648","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04648","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Buyuan","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Buyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiyu","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Shiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiping","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Yiping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanming","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yuanming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kang Hao","familyName":"Cheong","name":"Cheong, Kang Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EduPersona: Benchmarking Subjective Ability Boundaries of Virtual Student Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T09:52:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:23:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As large language models are increasingly integrated into education, virtual student agents are becoming vital for classroom simulation and teacher training. Yet their classroom-oriented subjective abilities remain largely unassessed, limiting understanding of model boundaries and hindering trustworthy deployment. We present EduPersona, a large-scale benchmark spanning two languages, three subjects, and ten persona types based on the Big Five theory. The dataset contains 1,308 authentic classroom dialogue rounds, corresponding to 12,814 teacher-student Q\u0026amp;A turns, and is further expanded through persona stylization into roughly 10 times larger scale (128k turns), providing a solid foundation for evaluation. Building on this resource, we decompose hard-to-quantify subjective performance into three progressive tasks: TASK1 basic coherence (whether behavior, emotion, expression, and voice align with classroom context), TASK2 student realism, and TASK3 long-term persona consistency, thereby establishing an evaluation framework grounded in educational theory and research value. We conduct systematic experiments on three representative LLMs, comparing their original versions with ten persona-fine-tuned variants trained on EduPersona. Results show consistent and significant average improvements across all tasks: TASK1 +33.6%, TASK2 +30.6%, and TASK3 +14.9%. These improvements highlight the dataset's effectiveness and research value, while also revealing the heterogeneous difficulty of persona modeling. In summary, EduPersona delivers the first classroom benchmark centered on subjective abilities, establishes a decoupled and verifiable research paradigm, and we will open-source both the dataset and the framework to support the broader research community in advancing trustworthy and human-like AI for education."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Preprint, Under review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04648","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:19:37Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:19:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:19:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04643","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04643","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04643","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangyu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiangyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yawen","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Yawen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaofen","familyName":"Xing","name":"Xing, Xiaofen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jin","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Jin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangmin","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Xiangmin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"QuantAgents: Towards Multi-agent Financial System via Simulated Trading"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T09:45:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:23:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this paper, our objective is to develop a multi-agent financial system that incorporates simulated trading, a technique extensively utilized by financial professionals. While current LLM-based agent models demonstrate competitive performance, they still exhibit significant deviations from real-world fund companies. A critical distinction lies in the agents' reliance on ``post-reflection'', particularly in response to adverse outcomes, but lack a distinctly human capability: long-term prediction of future trends. Therefore, we introduce QuantAgents, a multi-agent system integrating simulated trading, to comprehensively evaluate various investment strategies and market scenarios without assuming actual risks. Specifically, QuantAgents comprises four agents: a simulated trading analyst, a risk control analyst, a market news analyst, and a manager, who collaborate through several meetings. Moreover, our system incentivizes agents to receive feedback on two fronts: performance in real-world markets and predictive accuracy in simulated trading. Extensive experiments demonstrate that our framework excels across all metrics, yielding an overall return of nearly 300% over the three years (https://quantagents.github.io/)."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This paper has been accepted by EMNLP 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04643","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:19:30Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:19:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:19:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04637","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04637","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04637","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeyi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zeyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanju","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yanju","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heyuan","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Heyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tenglong","familyName":"Ao","name":"Ao, Tenglong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaohang","familyName":"Zhan","name":"Zhan, Xiaohang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Libin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Libin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Social Agent: Mastering Dyadic Nonverbal Behavior Generation via Conversational LLM Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Graphics (cs.GR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T09:41:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:23:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3757377.3763879","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present Social Agent, a novel framework for synthesizing realistic and contextually appropriate co-speech nonverbal behaviors in dyadic conversations. In this framework, we develop an agentic system driven by a Large Language Model (LLM) to direct the conversation flow and determine appropriate interactive behaviors for both participants. Additionally, we propose a novel dual-person gesture generation model based on an auto-regressive diffusion model, which synthesizes coordinated motions from speech signals. The output of the agentic system is translated into high-level guidance for the gesture generator, resulting in realistic movement at both the behavioral and motion levels. Furthermore, the agentic system periodically examines the movements of interlocutors and infers their intentions, forming a continuous feedback loop that enables dynamic and responsive interactions between the two participants. User studies and quantitative evaluations show that our model significantly improves the quality of dyadic interactions, producing natural, synchronized nonverbal behaviors."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"SIGGRAPH ASIA 2025 (Conference Track); Project page: https://pku-mocca.github.io/Social-Agent-Page/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04637","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-07T03:19:21Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:19:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:19:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04623","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04623","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04623","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shrish Shrinath","familyName":"Vaidya","name":"Vaidya, Shrish Shrinath","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gowthamaan","familyName":"Palani","name":"Palani, Gowthamaan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sidharth","familyName":"Ramesh","name":"Ramesh, Sidharth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Velmurugan","familyName":"Balasubramanian","name":"Balasubramanian, Velmurugan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minmini","familyName":"Selvam","name":"Selvam, Minmini","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gokulraja","familyName":"Srinivasaraja","name":"Srinivasaraja, Gokulraja","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ganapathy","familyName":"Krishnamurthi","name":"Krishnamurthi, Ganapathy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MedPAO: A Protocol-Driven Agent for Structuring Medical Reports"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T09:32:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:22:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1007/978-3-032-06004-4_4","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The deployment of Large Language Models (LLMs) for structuring clinical data is critically hindered by their tendency to hallucinate facts and their inability to follow domain-specific rules. To address this, we introduce MedPAO, a novel agentic framework that ensures accuracy and verifiable reasoning by grounding its operation in established clinical protocols such as the ABCDEF protocol for CXR analysis. MedPAO decomposes the report structuring task into a transparent process managed by a Plan-Act-Observe (PAO) loop and specialized tools. This protocol-driven method provides a verifiable alternative to opaque, monolithic models. The efficacy of our approach is demonstrated through rigorous evaluation: MedPAO achieves an F1-score of 0.96 on the critical sub-task of concept categorization. Notably, expert radiologists and clinicians rated the final structured outputs with an average score of 4.52 out of 5, indicating a level of reliability that surpasses baseline approaches relying solely on LLM-based foundation models. The code is available at: https://github.com/MiRL-IITM/medpao-agent"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Paper published at \"Agentic AI for Medicine\" Workshop, MICCAI 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04623","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-07T03:19:00Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:19:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:19:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04568","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04568","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04568","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Naman","familyName":"Gupta","name":"Gupta, Naman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shreeyash","familyName":"Gowaikar","name":"Gowaikar, Shreeyash","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arun","familyName":"Iyer","name":"Iyer, Arun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kirankumar","familyName":"Shiragur","name":"Shiragur, Kirankumar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ramakrishna B","familyName":"Bairi","name":"Bairi, Ramakrishna B","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rishikesh","familyName":"Maurya","name":"Maurya, Rishikesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ritabrata","familyName":"Maiti","name":"Maiti, Ritabrata","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sankarshan","familyName":"Damle","name":"Damle, Sankarshan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shachee Mishra","familyName":"Gupta","name":"Gupta, Shachee Mishra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"COSMIR: Chain Orchestrated Structured Memory for Iterative Reasoning over Long Context"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T08:10:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:19:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reasoning over very long inputs remains difficult for large language models (LLMs). Common workarounds either shrink the input via retrieval (risking missed evidence), enlarge the context window (straining selectivity), or stage multiple agents to read in pieces. In staged pipelines (e.g., Chain of Agents, CoA), free-form summaries passed between agents can discard crucial details and amplify early mistakes. We introduce COSMIR (Chain Orchestrated Structured Memory for Iterative Reasoning), a chain-style framework that replaces ad hoc messages with a structured memory. A Planner agent first turns a user query into concrete, checkable sub-questions. worker agents process chunks via a fixed micro-cycle: Extract, Infer, Refine, writing all updates to the shared memory. A Manager agent then Synthesizes the final answer directly from the memory. This preserves step-wise read-then-reason benefits while changing both the communication medium (structured memory) and the worker procedure (fixed micro-cycle), yielding higher faithfulness, better long-range aggregation, and auditability. On long-context QA from the HELMET suite, COSMIR reduces propagation-stage information loss and improves accuracy over a CoA baseline."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04568","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:17:41Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:17:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:17:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04560","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04560","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04560","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Honghao","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Honghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Ouyang","name":"Ouyang, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai-Wei","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Kai-Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiwei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zi","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Zi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yujun","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Yujun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ContextNav: Towards Agentic Multimodal In-Context Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T07:49:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:18:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances demonstrate that multimodal large language models (MLLMs) exhibit strong multimodal in-context learning (ICL) capabilities, enabling them to adapt to novel vision-language tasks from a few contextual examples. However, existing ICL approaches face challenges in reconciling scalability with robustness across diverse tasks and noisy contextual examples: manually selecting examples produces clean contexts but is labor-intensive and task-specific, while similarity-based retrieval improves scalability but could introduce irrelevant or structurally inconsistent samples that degrade ICL performance. To address these limitations, we propose ContextNav, the first agentic framework that integrates the scalability of automated retrieval with the quality and adaptiveness of human-like curation, enabling noise-robust and dynamically optimized contextualization for multimodal ICL. ContextNav unifies context management and noise-robust contextualization within a closed-loop workflow driven by graph-based orchestration. Specifically, it builds a resource-aware multimodal embedding pipeline, maintains a retrievable vector database, and applies agentic retrieval and structural alignment to construct noise-resilient contexts. An Operational Grammar Graph (OGG) further supports adaptive workflow planning and optimization, enabling the agent to refine its operational strategies based on downstream ICL feedback. Experimental results demonstrate that ContextNav achieves state-of-the-art performance across various datasets, underscoring the promise of agentic workflows for advancing scalable and robust contextualization in multimodal ICL."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04560","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:17:30Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:17:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:17:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04542","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04542","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04542","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wolfgang","familyName":"Lehrach","name":"Lehrach, Wolfgang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Hennes","name":"Hennes, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Miguel","familyName":"Lazaro-Gredilla","name":"Lazaro-Gredilla, Miguel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinghua","familyName":"Lou","name":"Lou, Xinghua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Carter","familyName":"Wendelken","name":"Wendelken, Carter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zun","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Antoine","familyName":"Dedieu","name":"Dedieu, Antoine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jordi","familyName":"Grau-Moya","name":"Grau-Moya, Jordi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marc","familyName":"Lanctot","name":"Lanctot, Marc","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Atil","familyName":"Iscen","name":"Iscen, Atil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"John","familyName":"Schultz","name":"Schultz, John","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marcus","familyName":"Chiam","name":"Chiam, Marcus","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ian","familyName":"Gemp","name":"Gemp, Ian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Piotr","familyName":"Zielinski","name":"Zielinski, Piotr","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Satinder","familyName":"Singh","name":"Singh, Satinder","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kevin P.","familyName":"Murphy","name":"Murphy, Kevin P.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Code World Models for General Game Playing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T07:16:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:17:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) reasoning abilities are increasingly being applied to classical board and card games, but the dominant approach -- involving prompting for direct move generation -- has significant drawbacks. It relies on the model's implicit fragile pattern-matching capabilities, leading to frequent illegal moves and strategically shallow play. Here we introduce an alternative approach: We use the LLM to translate natural language rules and game trajectories into a formal, executable world model represented as Python code. This generated model -- comprising functions for state transition, legal move enumeration, and termination checks -- serves as a verifiable simulation engine for high-performance planning algorithms like Monte Carlo tree search (MCTS). In addition, we prompt the LLM to generate heuristic value functions (to make MCTS more efficient), and inference functions (to estimate hidden states in imperfect information games). Our method offers three distinct advantages compared to directly using the LLM as a policy: (1) Verifiability: The generated CWM serves as a formal specification of the game's rules, allowing planners to algorithmically enumerate valid actions and avoid illegal moves, contingent on the correctness of the synthesized model; (2) Strategic Depth: We combine LLM semantic understanding with the deep search power of classical planners; and (3) Generalization: We direct the LLM to focus on the meta-task of data-to-code translation, enabling it to adapt to new games more easily. We evaluate our agent on 10 different games, of which 4 are novel and created for this paper. 5 of the games are fully observed (perfect information), and 5 are partially observed (imperfect information). We find that our method outperforms or matches Gemini 2.5 Pro in 9 out of the 10 considered games."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04542","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:17:03Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:17:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:17:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04536","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04536","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04536","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shun-ichiro","familyName":"Hayashi","name":"Hayashi, Shun-ichiro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daichi","familyName":"Mukunoki","name":"Mukunoki, Daichi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tetsuya","familyName":"Hoshino","name":"Hoshino, Tetsuya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Satoshi","familyName":"Ohshima","name":"Ohshima, Satoshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Takahiro","familyName":"Katagiri","name":"Katagiri, Takahiro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"3Dify: a Framework for Procedural 3D-CG Generation Assisted by LLMs Using MCP and RAG"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Graphics (cs.GR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T07:00:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:17:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper proposes \"3Dify,\" a procedural 3D computer graphics (3D-CG) generation framework utilizing Large Language Models (LLMs). The framework enables users to generate 3D-CG content solely through natural language instructions. 3Dify is built upon Dify, an open-source platform for AI application development, and incorporates several state-of-the-art LLM-related technologies such as the Model Context Protocol (MCP) and Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG). For 3D-CG generation support, 3Dify automates the operation of various Digital Content Creation (DCC) tools via MCP. When DCC tools do not support MCP-based interaction, the framework employs the Computer-Using Agent (CUA) method to automate Graphical User Interface (GUI) operations. Moreover, to enhance image generation quality, 3Dify allows users to provide feedback by selecting preferred images from multiple candidates. The LLM then learns variable patterns from these selections and applies them to subsequent generations. Furthermore, 3Dify supports the integration of locally deployed LLMs, enabling users to utilize custom-developed models and to reduce both time and monetary costs associated with external API calls by leveraging their own computational resources."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04536","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:16:55Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:16:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:16:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04519","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04519","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04519","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heiko","familyName":"Koziolek","name":"Koziolek, Heiko","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thilo","familyName":"Braun","name":"Braun, Thilo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Virendra","familyName":"Ashiwal","name":"Ashiwal, Virendra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sofia","familyName":"Linsbauer","name":"Linsbauer, Sofia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marthe Ahlgreen","familyName":"Hansen","name":"Hansen, Marthe Ahlgreen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Karoline","familyName":"Grotterud","name":"Grotterud, Karoline","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Spec2Control: Automating PLC/DCS Control-Logic Engineering from Natural Language Requirements with LLMs - A Multi-Plant Evaluation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T06:21:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:16:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Distributed control systems (DCS) manage the automation for many industrial production processes (e.g., power plants, chemical refineries, steel mills). Programming the software for such systems remains a largely manual and tedious process, incurring costs of millions of dollars for extensive facilities. Large language models (LLMs) have been found helpful in generating DCS control logic, resulting in commercial copilot tools. Today, these tools are focused on textual notations, they provide limited automation, and have not been tested on large datasets with realistic test cases. We introduce Spec2Control, a highly automated LLM workflow to generate graphical control logic directly from natural language user requirements. Experiments using an open dataset with 10 control narratives and 65 complex test cases demonstrate that Spec2Control can successfully identify control strategies, can generate 98.6% of correct control strategy connections autonomously, and can save between 94-96% of human labor. Spec2Control is being integrated into commercial ABB engineering tools, but is also available as an open-source variant for independent validation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 9 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04519","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:16:29Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:16:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:16:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04506","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04506","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04506","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiashuo","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Jiashuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shixuan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Shixuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaochen","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Zhaochen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xianrui","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Xianrui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengcheng","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Pengcheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bowen","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Bowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peiran","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Peiran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weijia","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Weijia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiawei","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Jiawei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GRACE: Generative Representation Learning via Contrastive Policy Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T05:46:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:15:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Prevailing methods for training Large Language Models (LLMs) as text encoders rely on contrastive losses that treat the model as a black box function, discarding its generative and reasoning capabilities in favor of static embeddings. We introduce GRACE (Generative Representation Learning via Contrastive Policy Optimization), a novel framework that reimagines contrastive signals not as losses to be minimized, but as rewards that guide a generative policy. In GRACE, the LLM acts as a policy that produces explicit, human-interpretable rationales--structured natural language explanations of its semantic understanding. These rationales are then encoded into high-quality embeddings via mean pooling. Using policy gradient optimization, we train the model with a multi-component reward function that maximizes similarity between query positive pairs and minimizes similarity with negatives. This transforms the LLM from an opaque encoder into an interpretable agent whose reasoning process is transparent and inspectable. On MTEB benchmark, GRACE yields broad cross category gains: averaged over four backbones, the supervised setting improves overall score by 11.5% over base models, and the unsupervised variant adds 6.9%, while preserving general capabilities. This work treats contrastive objectives as rewards over rationales, unifying representation learning with generation to produce stronger embeddings and transparent rationales. The model, data and code are available at https://github.com/GasolSun36/GRACE."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"23 pages, 7 figures, 7 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04506","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:16:11Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:16:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:16:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04488","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04488","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04488","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Edward Y.","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Edward Y.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ethan Y.","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Ethan Y.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multi-Agent Collaborative Intelligence: Dual-Dial Control for Reliable LLM Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Theory (cs.IT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.4","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T04:52:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:14:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multi-agent debate often wastes compute by using a fixed adversarial stance, aggregating without deliberation, or stopping on heuristics. We introduce MACI, an active controller with two independent dials that decouple information from behavior: an information dial that gates evidence by quality, and a behavior dial that schedules contentiousness from exploration to consolidation. A moderator tracks disagreement, overlap, evidence quality, and argument quality, and halts when gains plateau. We provide theory-lite guarantees for nonincreasing dispersion and provable termination, with a budget-feasible scheduler. Across clinical diagnosis and news-bias tasks, MACI improves accuracy and calibration while reducing tokens, and converts residual uncertainty into precision RAG plans that specify what to retrieve next. We use a cross-family LLM judge (CRIT) as a conservative soft weight and stop signal, validated for order invariance and judge-swap stability; stability depends on using high-capability judges. MACI turns debate into a budget-aware, measurable, and provably terminating controller."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"27 pages, 5 figures, 21 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04488","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:15:44Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:15:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:15:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04463","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04463","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04463","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Takashi","familyName":"Maekaku","name":"Maekaku, Takashi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keita","familyName":"Goto","name":"Goto, Keita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinchuan","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Jinchuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yusuke","familyName":"Shinohara","name":"Shinohara, Yusuke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shinji","familyName":"Watanabe","name":"Watanabe, Shinji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Evaluating Self-Supervised Speech Models via Text-Based LLMS"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T03:25:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:13:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Self-Supervised Learning (SSL) has gained traction for its ability to learn rich representations with low labeling costs, applicable across diverse downstream tasks. However, assessing the downstream-task performance remains challenging due to the cost of extra training and evaluation. Existing methods for task-agnostic evaluation also require extra training or hyperparameter tuning. We propose a novel evaluation metric using large language models (LLMs). By inputting discrete token sequences and minimal domain cues derived from SSL models into LLMs, we obtain the mean log-likelihood; these cues guide in-context learning, rendering the score more reliable without extra training or hyperparameter tuning. Experimental results show a correlation between LLM-based scores and automatic speech recognition task. Additionally, our findings reveal that LLMs not only functions as an SSL evaluation tools but also provides inference-time embeddings that are useful for speaker verification task."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ASRU 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04463","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:15:08Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:15:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:15:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04392","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04392","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04392","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Faisal","familyName":"Hamman","name":"Hamman, Faisal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenyang","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Chenyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anoop","familyName":"Kumar","name":"Kumar, Anoop","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xujun","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Xujun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sanghamitra","familyName":"Dutta","name":"Dutta, Sanghamitra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daben","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Daben","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alfy","familyName":"Samuel","name":"Samuel, Alfy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Improving Consistency in Retrieval-Augmented Systems with Group Similarity Rewards"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T23:14:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:08:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"RAG systems are increasingly deployed in high-stakes domains where users expect outputs to be consistent across semantically equivalent queries. However, existing systems often exhibit significant inconsistencies due to variability in both the retriever and generator (LLM), undermining trust and reliability. In this work, we focus on information consistency, i.e., the requirement that outputs convey the same core content across semantically equivalent inputs. We introduce a principled evaluation framework that decomposes RAG consistency into retriever-level, generator-level, and end-to-end components, helping identify inconsistency sources. To improve consistency, we propose Paraphrased Set Group Relative Policy Optimization (PS-GRPO), an RL approach that leverages multiple rollouts across paraphrased set to assign group similarity rewards. We leverage PS-GRPO to achieve Information Consistent RAG (Con-RAG), training the generator to produce consistent outputs across paraphrased queries and remain robust to retrieval-induced variability. Because exact reward computation over paraphrase sets is computationally expensive, we also introduce a scalable approximation method that retains effectiveness while enabling efficient, large-scale training. Empirical evaluations across short-form, multi-hop, and long-form QA benchmarks demonstrate that Con-RAG significantly improves both consistency and accuracy over strong baselines, even in the absence of explicit ground-truth supervision. Our work provides practical solutions for evaluating and building reliable RAG systems for safety-critical deployments."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at NeurIPS 2025 Workshop on Reliable ML from Unreliable Data"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04392","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:13:23Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:13:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:13:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04364","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04364","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04364","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Morita","familyName":"Tarvirdians","name":"Tarvirdians, Morita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Senthil","familyName":"Chandrasegaran","name":"Chandrasegaran, Senthil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hayley","familyName":"Hung","name":"Hung, Hayley","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Catholijn M.","familyName":"Jonker","name":"Jonker, Catholijn M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Catharine","familyName":"Oertel","name":"Oertel, Catharine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Reflection Before Action: Designing a Framework for Quantifying Thought Patterns for Increased Self-awareness in Personal Decision Making"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T21:16:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:06:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"When making significant life decisions, people increasingly turn to conversational AI tools, such as large language models (LLMs). However, LLMs often steer users toward solutions, limiting metacognitive awareness of their own decision-making. In this paper, we shift the focus in decision support from solution-orientation to reflective activity, coining the term pre-decision reflection (PDR). We introduce PROBE, the first framework that assesses pre-decision reflections along two dimensions: breadth (diversity of thought categories) and depth (elaborateness of reasoning). Coder agreement demonstrates PROBE's reliability in capturing how people engage in pre-decision reflection. Our study reveals substantial heterogeneity across participants and shows that people perceived their unassisted reflections as deeper and broader than PROBE's measures. By surfacing hidden thought patterns, PROBE opens opportunities for technologies that foster self-awareness and strengthen people's agency in choosing which thought patterns to rely on in decision-making."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04364","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:12:42Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:12:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:12:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04341","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04341","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04341","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"G. Niklas","familyName":"Noren","name":"Noren, G. Niklas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eva-Lisa","familyName":"Meldau","name":"Meldau, Eva-Lisa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Johan","familyName":"Ellenius","name":"Ellenius, Johan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Critical appraisal of artificial intelligence for rare-event recognition: principles and pharmacovigilance case studies"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.0","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T20:05:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:05:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Many high-stakes AI applications target low-prevalence events, where apparent accuracy can conceal limited real-world value. Relevant AI models range from expert-defined rules and traditional machine learning to generative LLMs constrained for classification. We outline key considerations for critical appraisal of AI in rare-event recognition, including problem framing and test set design, prevalence-aware statistical evaluation, robustness assessment, and integration into human workflows. In addition, we propose an approach to structured case-level examination (SCLE), to complement statistical performance evaluation, and a comprehensive checklist to guide procurement or development of AI models for rare-event recognition. We instantiate the framework in pharmacovigilance, drawing on three studies: rule-based retrieval of pregnancy-related reports; duplicate detection combining machine learning with probabilistic record linkage; and automated redaction of person names using an LLM. We highlight pitfalls specific to the rare-event setting including optimism from unrealistic class balance and lack of difficult positive controls in test sets - and show how cost-sensitive targets align model performance with operational value. While grounded in pharmacovigilance practice, the principles generalize to domains where positives are scarce and error costs may be asymmetric."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"28 pages, 2 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04341","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:12:08Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:12:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:12:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04317","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04317","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04317","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yucong","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Yucong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Lu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Feng","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Feng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mashrur","familyName":"Chowdhury","name":"Chowdhury, Mashrur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongkai","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yongkai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FairAgent: Democratizing Fairness-Aware Machine Learning with LLM-Powered Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T18:33:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:04:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Training fair and unbiased machine learning models is crucial for high-stakes applications, yet it presents significant challenges. Effective bias mitigation requires deep expertise in fairness definitions, metrics, data preprocessing, and machine learning techniques. In addition, the complex process of balancing model performance with fairness requirements while properly handling sensitive attributes makes fairness-aware model development inaccessible to many practitioners. To address these challenges, we introduce FairAgent, an LLM-powered automated system that significantly simplifies fairness-aware model development. FairAgent eliminates the need for deep technical expertise by automatically analyzing datasets for potential biases, handling data preprocessing and feature engineering, and implementing appropriate bias mitigation strategies based on user requirements. Our experiments demonstrate that FairAgent achieves significant performance improvements while significantly reducing development time and expertise requirements, making fairness-aware machine learning more accessible to practitioners."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ICDM 2025 Demo Workshop"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04317","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:11:33Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:11:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:11:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04311","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04311","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04311","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bohan","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Bohan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huidong","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Huidong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keyue","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Keyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaowen","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Xiaowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On the Importance of Task Complexity in Evaluating LLM-Based Multi-Agent Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T18:08:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:03:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language model multi-agent systems (LLM-MAS) offer a promising paradigm for harnessing collective intelligence to achieve more advanced forms of AI behaviour. While recent studies suggest that LLM-MAS can outperform LLM single-agent systems (LLM-SAS) on certain tasks, the lack of systematic experimental designs limits the strength and generality of these conclusions. We argue that a principled understanding of task complexity, such as the degree of sequential reasoning required and the breadth of capabilities involved, is essential for assessing the effectiveness of LLM-MAS in task solving. To this end, we propose a theoretical framework characterising tasks along two dimensions: depth, representing reasoning length, and width, representing capability diversity. We theoretically examine a representative class of LLM-MAS, namely the multi-agent debate system, and empirically evaluate its performance in both discriminative and generative tasks with varying depth and width. Theoretical and empirical results show that the benefit of LLM-MAS over LLM-SAS increases with both task depth and width, and the effect is more pronounced with respect to depth. This clarifies when LLM-MAS are beneficial and provides a principled foundation for designing future LLM-MAS methods and benchmarks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04311","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:11:24Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:11:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:11:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04293","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04293","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04293","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingnan","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Lingnan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chong","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Chong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaiyuan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Kaiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liu","familyName":"Zhengyong","name":"Zhengyong, Liu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenqiang","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Wenqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fanqing","familyName":"Meng","name":"Meng, Fanqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Equipping Retrieval-Augmented Large Language Models with Document Structure Awareness"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T17:04:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:02:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While large language models (LLMs) demonstrate impressive capabilities, their reliance on parametric knowledge often leads to factual inaccuracies. Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) mitigates this by leveraging external documents, yet existing approaches treat retrieved passages as isolated chunks, ignoring valuable structure that is crucial for document organization. Motivated by this gap, we propose Retrieve-DocumentRoute-Read (RDR2), a novel framework that explicitly incorporates structural information throughout the RAG process. RDR2 employs an LLM-based router to dynamically navigate document structure trees, jointly evaluating content relevance and hierarchical relationships to assemble optimal evidence. Our key innovation lies in formulating document routing as a trainable task, with automatic action curation and structure-aware passage selection inspired by human reading strategies. Through comprehensive evaluation on five challenging datasets, RDR2 achieves state-of-the-art performance, demonstrating that explicit structural awareness significantly enhances RAG systems' ability to acquire and utilize knowledge, particularly in complex scenarios requiring multi-document synthesis."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EMNLP2025 Findings"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04293","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:10:58Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:10:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:10:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04257","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04257","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04257","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanjie","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yanjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiming","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Yiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AgentTypo: Adaptive Typographic Prompt Injection Attacks against Black-box Multimodal Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T15:46:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:00:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal agents built on large vision-language models (LVLMs) are increasingly deployed in open-world settings but remain highly vulnerable to prompt injection, especially through visual inputs. We introduce AgentTypo, a black-box red-teaming framework that mounts adaptive typographic prompt injection by embedding optimized text into webpage images. Our automatic typographic prompt injection (ATPI) algorithm maximizes prompt reconstruction by substituting captioners while minimizing human detectability via a stealth loss, with a Tree-structured Parzen Estimator guiding black-box optimization over text placement, size, and color. To further enhance attack strength, we develop AgentTypo-pro, a multi-LLM system that iteratively refines injection prompts using evaluation feedback and retrieves successful past examples for continual learning. Effective prompts are abstracted into generalizable strategies and stored in a strategy repository, enabling progressive knowledge accumulation and reuse in future attacks. Experiments on the VWA-Adv benchmark across Classifieds, Shopping, and Reddit scenarios show that AgentTypo significantly outperforms the latest image-based attacks such as AgentAttack. On GPT-4o agents, our image-only attack raises the success rate from 0.23 to 0.45, with consistent results across GPT-4V, GPT-4o-mini, Gemini 1.5 Pro, and Claude 3 Opus. In image+text settings, AgentTypo achieves 0.68 ASR, also outperforming the latest baselines. Our findings reveal that AgentTypo poses a practical and potent threat to multimodal agents and highlight the urgent need for effective defense."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages, 8 figures. Submitted to IEEE Transactions on Information Forensics \u0026amp; Security"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04257","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:10:06Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:10:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:10:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04239","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04239","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04239","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tongzhou","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Tongzhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yuhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maolin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Maolin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Chi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangyu","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Xiangyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Empowering Denoising Sequential Recommendation with Large Language Model Embeddings"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T15:10:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:59:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3746252.3761427","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Sequential recommendation aims to capture user preferences by modeling sequential patterns in user-item interactions. However, these models are often influenced by noise such as accidental interactions, leading to suboptimal performance. Therefore, to reduce the effect of noise, some works propose explicitly identifying and removing noisy items. However, we find that simply relying on collaborative information may result in an over-denoising problem, especially for cold items. To overcome these limitations, we propose a novel framework: Interest Alignment for Denoising Sequential Recommendation (IADSR) which integrates both collaborative and semantic information. Specifically, IADSR is comprised of two stages: in the first stage, we obtain the collaborative and semantic embeddings of each item from a traditional sequential recommendation model and an LLM, respectively. In the second stage, we align the collaborative and semantic embeddings and then identify noise in the interaction sequence based on long-term and short-term interests captured in the collaborative and semantic modalities. Our extensive experiments on four public datasets validate the effectiveness of the proposed framework and its compatibility with different sequential recommendation systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by CIKM2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04239","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-07T03:09:39Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:09:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:09:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04206","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04206","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04206","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanchen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Hanchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bowen","familyName":"Lv","name":"Lv, Bowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xueqiao","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Xueqiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bohao","familyName":"Jing","name":"Jing, Bohao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Iat Long","familyName":"Iong","name":"Iong, Iat Long","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenyu","familyName":"Hou","name":"Hou, Zhenyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zehan","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Zehan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanyu","familyName":"Lai","name":"Lai, Hanyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifan","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Yifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongning","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Hongning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxiao","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Yuxiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AgentRL: Scaling Agentic Reinforcement Learning with a Multi-Turn, Multi-Task Framework"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T13:40:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:57:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) have sparked growing interest in building generalist agents that can learn through online interactions. However, applying reinforcement learning (RL) to train LLM agents in multi-turn, multi-task settings remains challenging due to lack of scalable infrastructure and stable training algorithms. In this work, we present the AgentRL framework for scalable multi-turn, multi-task agentic RL training. On the infrastructure side, AgentRL features a fully-asynchronous generation-training pipeline for efficient multi-turn RL. To support heterogeneous environment development in multi-task RL, we design a unified function-call based API interface, containerized environment development, and a centralized controller. On the algorithm side, we propose cross-policy sampling to encourage model exploration in multi-turn settings and task advantage normalization to stabilize multi-task training. Experiments show that AgentRL, trained on open LLMs across five agentic tasks, significantly outperforms GPT-5, Clause-Sonnet-4, DeepSeek-R1, and other open-source LLM agents. Multi-task training with AgentRL matches the best results among all task-specific models. AgentRL is open-sourced at https://github.com/THUDM/AgentRL. The algorithm and framework are adopted in building \\textsc{\\href{https://autoglm.zhipuai.cn}{AutoGLM}}."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04206","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:08:49Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:08:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:08:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04202","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04202","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04202","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haiquan","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Haiquan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"You","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, You","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingjie","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Yingjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaqing","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yaqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Quanming","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Quanming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Spectral Alignment as Predictor of Loss Explosion in Neural Network Training"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T13:35:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:57:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Loss explosions in training deep neural networks can nullify multi-million dollar training runs. Conventional monitoring metrics like weight and gradient norms are often lagging and ambiguous predictors, as their values vary dramatically across different models and even between layers of the same model, making it difficult to establish a unified standard for detecting impending failure. We introduce Spectral Alignment (SA), a novel, theoretically-grounded metric that monitors the distributional alignment between layer inputs and the principal singular vectors of weight matrices. We show that a collapse in the sign diversity of this alignment is a powerful early predictor of representational collapse and training divergence. Empirical results on language models demonstrate that monitoring the SA distribution provides a significantly earlier and clearer warning of loss explosions than traditional scalar metrics. SA's low computational overhead makes it a practical tool for safeguarding model training."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages, 8 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04202","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:08:43Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:08:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:08:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04147","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04147","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04147","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifeng","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Yifeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziang","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Ziang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxuan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yuxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Biqing","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Biqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanlin","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Hanlin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linfeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Linfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Self Speculative Decoding for Diffusion Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T10:52:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:54:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion-based Large Language Models (dLLMs) have emerged as a competitive alternative to autoregressive models, offering unique advantages through bidirectional attention and parallel generation paradigms. However, the generation results of current parallel decoding methods deviate from stepwise decoding, introducing potential performance degradation, which limits their practical deployment. To address this problem, we propose \\textbf{S}elf \\textbf{S}peculative \\textbf{D}ecoding (SSD), a lossless inference acceleration method that leverages the dLLM itself as both speculative decoding drafter and verifier without auxiliary modules. SSD introduces a self-drafting mechanism where the model generates predictions for multiple positions, then verifies them through hierarchical verification trees in a single forward pass. Unlike traditional speculative decoding that requires separate draft models, SSD eliminates model redundancy and memory overhead by exploiting the dLLM's inherent parallel prediction capability for multiple positions. This self-speculative approach allows the model to progressively verify and accept multiple tokens in a single forward pass. Our experiments demonstrate that SSD achieves up to 3.46$\\times$ speedup while keeping the output identical to stepwise decoding on open source models such as LLaDA and Dream. Code will be made publicly available on GitHub."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04147","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:07:23Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:07:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:07:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04145","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04145","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04145","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenxin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chenxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Elyas Asadi","familyName":"Shamsabadi","name":"Shamsabadi, Elyas Asadi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaohui","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zhaohui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luming","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Luming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alireza Ahmadian Fard","familyName":"Fini","name":"Fini, Alireza Ahmadian Fard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Dias-da-Costa","name":"Dias-da-Costa, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Automating construction safety inspections using a multi-modal vision-language RAG framework"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T10:48:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:54:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Conventional construction safety inspection methods are often inefficient as they require navigating through large volume of information. Recent advances in large vision-language models (LVLMs) provide opportunities to automate safety inspections through enhanced visual and linguistic understanding. However, existing applications face limitations including irrelevant or unspecific responses, restricted modal inputs and hallucinations. Utilisation of Large Language Models (LLMs) for this purpose is constrained by availability of training data and frequently lack real-time adaptability. This study introduces SiteShield, a multi-modal LVLM-based Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) framework for automating construction safety inspection reports by integrating visual and audio inputs. Using real-world data, SiteShield outperformed unimodal LLMs without RAG with an F1 score of 0.82, hamming loss of 0.04, precision of 0.76, and recall of 0.96. The findings indicate that SiteShield offers a novel pathway to enhance information retrieval and efficiency in generating safety reports."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"33 pages, 11 figures, 7 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04145","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:07:19Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:07:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:07:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04135","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04135","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04135","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingzhi","familyName":"Gong","name":"Gong, Jingzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yixin","familyName":"Bian","name":"Bian, Yixin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luis","familyName":"de la Cal","name":"de la Cal, Luis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giovanni","familyName":"Pinna","name":"Pinna, Giovanni","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anisha","familyName":"Uteem","name":"Uteem, Anisha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Williams","name":"Williams, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mar","familyName":"Zamorano","name":"Zamorano, Mar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Karine","familyName":"Even-Mendoza","name":"Even-Mendoza, Karine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"W. B.","familyName":"Langdon","name":"Langdon, W. B.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hector","familyName":"Menendez","name":"Menendez, Hector","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Federica","familyName":"Sarro","name":"Sarro, Federica","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GA4GC: Greener Agent for Greener Code via Multi-Objective Configuration Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T10:34:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:54:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Coding agents powered by LLMs face critical sustainability and scalability challenges in industrial deployment, with single runs consuming over 100k tokens and incurring environmental costs that may exceed optimization benefits. This paper introduces GA4GC, the first framework to systematically optimize coding agent runtime (greener agent) and code performance (greener code) trade-offs by discovering Pareto-optimal agent hyperparameters and prompt templates. Evaluation on the SWE-Perf benchmark demonstrates up to 135x hypervolume improvement, reducing agent runtime by 37.7% while improving correctness. Our findings establish temperature as the most critical hyperparameter, and provide actionable strategies to balance agent sustainability with code optimization effectiveness in industrial deployment."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by SSBSE'25 Challenge Track"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04135","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:07:04Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:07:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:07:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04096","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04096","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04096","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tommy","familyName":"Mordo","name":"Mordo, Tommy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sagie","familyName":"Dekel","name":"Dekel, Sagie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Omer","familyName":"Madmon","name":"Madmon, Omer","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Moshe","familyName":"Tennenholtz","name":"Tennenholtz, Moshe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oren","familyName":"Kurland","name":"Kurland, Oren","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RLRF: Competitive Search Agent Design via Reinforcement Learning from Ranker Feedback"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Science and Game Theory (cs.GT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T08:46:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:51:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Competitive search is a setting where document publishers modify them to improve their ranking in response to a query. Recently, publishers have increasingly leveraged LLMs to generate and modify competitive content. We introduce Reinforcement Learning from Ranker Feedback (RLRF), a framework that trains LLMs using preference datasets derived from ranking competitions. The goal of a publisher (LLM-based) agent is to optimize content for improved ranking while accounting for the strategies of competing agents. We generate the datasets using approaches that do not rely on human-authored data. We show that our proposed agents consistently and substantially outperform previously suggested approaches for LLM-based competitive document modification. We further show that our agents are effective with ranking functions they were not trained for (i.e., out of distribution) and they adapt to strategic opponents. These findings provide support to the significant potential of using reinforcement learning in competitive search."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04096","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:06:07Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:06:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:06:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04078","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04078","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04078","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Minn","name":"Minn, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yonghui","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yonghui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiakun","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jiakun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ferdian","familyName":"Thung","name":"Thung, Ferdian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Terry Yue","familyName":"Zhuo","name":"Zhuo, Terry Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lwin Khin","familyName":"Shar","name":"Shar, Lwin Khin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Debin","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Debin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Lo","name":"Lo, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Bamboo: LLM-Driven Discovery of API-Permission Mappings in the Android Framework"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T07:50:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:50:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The permission mechanism in the Android Framework is integral to safeguarding the privacy of users by managing users' and processes' access to sensitive resources and operations. As such, developers need to be equipped with an in-depth understanding of API permissions to build robust Android apps. Unfortunately, the official API documentation by Android chronically suffers from imprecision and incompleteness, causing developers to spend significant effort to accurately discern necessary permissions. This potentially leads to incorrect permission declarations in Android app development, potentially resulting in security violations and app failures. Recent efforts in improving permission specification primarily leverage static and dynamic code analyses to uncover API-permission mappings within the Android framework. Yet, these methodologies encounter substantial shortcomings, including poor adaptability to Android SDK and Framework updates, restricted code coverage, and a propensity to overlook essential API-permission mappings in intricate codebases. This paper introduces a pioneering approach utilizing large language models (LLMs) for a systematic examination of API-permission mappings. In addition to employing LLMs, we integrate a dual-role prompting strategy and an API-driven code generation approach into our mapping discovery pipeline, resulting in the development of the corresponding tool, \\tool{}. We formulate three research questions to evaluate the efficacy of \\tool{} against state-of-the-art baselines, assess the completeness of official SDK documentation, and analyze the evolution of permission-required APIs across different SDK releases. Our experimental results reveal that \\tool{} identifies 2,234, 3,552, and 4,576 API-permission mappings in Android versions 6, 7, and 10 respectively, substantially outprforming existing baselines."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04078","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:05:39Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:05:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:05:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04076","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04076","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04076","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amin","familyName":"Vahidi-Moghaddam","name":"Vahidi-Moghaddam, Amin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sayed Pedram Haeri","familyName":"Boroujeni","name":"Boroujeni, Sayed Pedram Haeri","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Iman","familyName":"Jebellat","name":"Jebellat, Iman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ehsan","familyName":"Jebellat","name":"Jebellat, Ehsan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Niloufar","familyName":"Mehrabi","name":"Mehrabi, Niloufar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaojian","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhaojian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Shadow to Light: Toward Safe and Efficient Policy Learning Across MPC, DeePC, RL, and LLM Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T07:47:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:50:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal","rightsIdentifier":"cc0-1.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"One of the main challenges in modern control applications, particularly in robot and vehicle motion control, is achieving accurate, fast, and safe movement. To address this, optimal control policies have been developed to enforce safety while ensuring high performance. Since basic first-principles models of real systems are often available, model-based controllers are widely used. Model predictive control (MPC) is a leading approach that optimizes performance while explicitly handling safety constraints. However, obtaining accurate models for complex systems is difficult, which motivates data-driven alternatives. ML-based MPC leverages learned models to reduce reliance on hand-crafted dynamics, while reinforcement learning (RL) can learn near-optimal policies directly from interaction data. Data-enabled predictive control (DeePC) goes further by bypassing modeling altogether, directly learning safe policies from raw input-output data. Recently, large language model (LLM) agents have also emerged, translating natural language instructions into structured formulations of optimal control problems. Despite these advances, data-driven policies face significant limitations. They often suffer from slow response times, high computational demands, and large memory needs, making them less practical for real-world systems with fast dynamics, limited onboard computing, or strict memory constraints. To address this, various technique, such as reduced-order modeling, function-approximated policy learning, and convex relaxations, have been proposed to reduce computational complexity. In this paper, we present eight such approaches and demonstrate their effectiveness across real-world applications, including robotic arms, soft robots, and vehicle motion control."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04076","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:05:36Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:05:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:05:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04039","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04039","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04039","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Lei","name":"Lei, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nuo","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Nuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ali","familyName":"Payani","name":"Payani, Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingyi","familyName":"Hong","name":"Hong, Mingyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunhua","familyName":"Liao","name":"Liao, Chunhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Caiwen","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Caiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"\\textsc{GUI-Spotlight}: Adaptive Iterative Focus Refinement for Enhanced GUI Visual Grounding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T05:15:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:48:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal large language models (MLLMs) have markedly expanded the competence of graphical user-interface (GUI) systems, propelling them beyond controlled simulations into complex, real-world environments across diverse platforms. However, practical usefulness is still bounded by the reliability of visual grounding, i.e., mapping textual references to exact on-screen elements. This limitation prevents the system from accurately performing pointer-level actions such as clicking or dragging. To address it, we introduce GUI-Spotlight -- a model trained for image-grounded reasoning that dynamically invokes multiple specialized tools to iteratively narrow its focus to the relevant region of the screen, thereby substantially improving visual grounding accuracy. On the ScreenSpot-Pro benchmark, GUI-Spotlight trained with only 18.5K training samples achieves 52.8\\% accuracy, surpassing V2P-7B (50.6\\% with 9.6M training samples) and GTA-1-7B (50.1\\% with 1.56M training samples)."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04039","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:04:41Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:04:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:04:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04023","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04023","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04023","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mizanur","familyName":"Rahman","name":"Rahman, Mizanur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amran","familyName":"Bhuiyan","name":"Bhuiyan, Amran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammed Saidul","familyName":"Islam","name":"Islam, Mohammed Saidul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Md Tahmid Rahman","familyName":"Laskar","name":"Laskar, Md Tahmid Rahman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ridwan","familyName":"Mahbub","name":"Mahbub, Ridwan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ahmed","familyName":"Masry","name":"Masry, Ahmed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shafiq","familyName":"Joty","name":"Joty, Shafiq","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Enamul","familyName":"Hoque","name":"Hoque, Enamul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LLM-Based Data Science Agents: A Survey of Capabilities, Challenges, and Future Directions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T04:04:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:47:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) have enabled a new class of AI agents that automate multiple stages of the data science workflow by integrating planning, tool use, and multimodal reasoning across text, code, tables, and visuals. This survey presents the first comprehensive, lifecycle-aligned taxonomy of data science agents, systematically analyzing and mapping forty-five systems onto the six stages of the end-to-end data science process: business understanding and data acquisition, exploratory analysis and visualization, feature engineering, model building and selection, interpretation and explanation, and deployment and monitoring. In addition to lifecycle coverage, we annotate each agent along five cross-cutting design dimensions: reasoning and planning style, modality integration, tool orchestration depth, learning and alignment methods, and trust, safety, and governance mechanisms. Beyond classification, we provide a critical synthesis of agent capabilities, highlight strengths and limitations at each stage, and review emerging benchmarks and evaluation practices. Our analysis identifies three key trends: most systems emphasize exploratory analysis, visualization, and modeling while neglecting business understanding, deployment, and monitoring; multimodal reasoning and tool orchestration remain unresolved challenges; and over 90% lack explicit trust and safety mechanisms. We conclude by outlining open challenges in alignment stability, explainability, governance, and robust evaluation frameworks, and propose future research directions to guide the development of robust, trustworthy, low-latency, transparent, and broadly accessible data science agents."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Survey paper; 45 data science agents; under review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04023","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:04:18Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:04:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:04:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04016","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04016","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04016","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thanapol","familyName":"Popit","name":"Popit, Thanapol","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Natthapath","familyName":"Rungseesiripak","name":"Rungseesiripak, Natthapath","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Monthol","familyName":"Charattrakool","name":"Charattrakool, Monthol","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saksorn","familyName":"Ruangtanusak","name":"Ruangtanusak, Saksorn","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Thai Semantic End-of-Turn Detection for Real-Time Voice Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T03:31:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:46:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Fluid voice-to-voice interaction requires reliable and low-latency detection of when a user has finished speaking. Traditional audio-silence end-pointers add hundreds of milliseconds of delay and fail under hesitations or language-specific phenomena. We present, to our knowledge, the first systematic study of Thai text-only end-of-turn (EOT) detection for real-time agents. We compare zero-shot and few-shot prompting of compact LLMs to supervised fine-tuning of lightweight transformers. Using transcribed subtitles from the YODAS corpus and Thai-specific linguistic cues (e.g., sentence-final particles), we formulate EOT as a binary decision over token boundaries. We report a clear accuracy-latency tradeoff and provide a public-ready implementation plan. This work establishes a Thai baseline and demonstrates that small, fine-tuned models can deliver near-instant EOT decisions suitable for on-device agents."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"IEEE ICSEC 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04016","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:04:08Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:04:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:04:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03984","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03984","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03984","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kirandeep","familyName":"Kaur","name":"Kaur, Kirandeep","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Preetam Prabhu Srikar","familyName":"Dammu","name":"Dammu, Preetam Prabhu Srikar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hideo","familyName":"Joho","name":"Joho, Hideo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chirag","familyName":"Shah","name":"Shah, Chirag","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Beyond Static Evaluation: Rethinking the Assessment of Personalized Agent Adaptability in Information Retrieval"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T00:35:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:44:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3767695.3769484","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Personalized AI agents are becoming central to modern information retrieval, yet most evaluation methodologies remain static, relying on fixed benchmarks and one-off metrics that fail to reflect how users' needs evolve over time. These limitations hinder our ability to assess whether agents can meaningfully adapt to individuals across dynamic, longitudinal interactions. In this perspective paper, we propose a conceptual lens for rethinking evaluation in adaptive personalization, shifting the focus from static performance snapshots to interaction-aware, evolving assessments. We organize this lens around three core components: (1) persona-based user simulation with temporally evolving preference models; (2) structured elicitation protocols inspired by reference interviews to extract preferences in context; and (3) adaptation-aware evaluation mechanisms that measure how agent behavior improves across sessions and tasks. While recent works have embraced LLM-driven user simulation, we situate this practice within a broader paradigm for evaluating agents over time. To illustrate our ideas, we conduct a case study in e-commerce search using the PersonalWAB dataset. Beyond presenting a framework, our work lays a conceptual foundation for understanding and evaluating personalization as a continuous, user-centric endeavor."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03984","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-07T03:03:21Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:03:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:03:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03978","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03978","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03978","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Min Woo","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Min Woo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alejandro","familyName":"Lozano","name":"Lozano, Alejandro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Javier Gamazo","familyName":"Tejero","name":"Tejero, Javier Gamazo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vishwesh","familyName":"Nath","name":"Nath, Vishwesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao Xiao","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Xiao Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James","familyName":"Burgess","name":"Burgess, James","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhui","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yuhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kun","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Kun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robert","familyName":"Tibshirani","name":"Tibshirani, Robert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sean","familyName":"Huver","name":"Huver, Sean","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Serena","familyName":"Yeung-Levy","name":"Yeung-Levy, Serena","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"No Tokens Wasted: Leveraging Long Context in Biomedical Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T23:38:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:44:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Embedding vision-language models (VLMs) are typically pretrained with short text windows (\u0026lt;77 tokens), which forces the truncation of long-format captions. Yet, the distribution of biomedical captions from large-scale open source literature reveals that a huge portion of captions far exceed 77 tokens. To this end, we investigate the impact of pretraining on long-format biomedical captions by extending the context length of text encoders in VLMs. We find that longer context (thus, enabling additional supervision provided in long-format captions) correlates with better retrieval and classification performance. Given this finding, we introduce BIOMEDICA-LongCAP, a dataset of 1M image-caption pairs enriched with context-aware descriptions from full-text articles, providing longer and additional textual supervision. Using BIOMEDICA-LongCAP, we train BMC-LongCLIP, a long-context biomedical VLM with a text encoder supporting windows of up to 512 tokens. Our model extends context capacity by 6.6x, reducing token waste from 55% to just 2.2%. On long-caption retrieval benchmarks, BMC-LongCLIP achieves up to +30% absolute gains in Recall@1 and +2% average improvements in classification, while also converging faster than short-context. Our results demonstrate that long-context modeling is a promising direction for advancing biomedical VLMs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03978","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:03:12Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:03:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:03:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03921","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03921","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03921","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arushi","familyName":"Dashore","name":"Dashore, Arushi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aryan","familyName":"Anumala","name":"Anumala, Aryan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emily","familyName":"Hui","name":"Hui, Emily","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Olivia","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Olivia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Talking Tennis: Language Feedback from 3D Biomechanical Action Recognition"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.10; I.5.4; I.2.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T19:55:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:41:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Automated tennis stroke analysis has advanced significantly with the integration of biomechanical motion cues alongside deep learning techniques, enhancing stroke classification accuracy and player performance evaluation. Despite these advancements, existing systems often fail to connect biomechanical insights with actionable language feedback that is both accessible and meaningful to players and coaches. This research project addresses this gap by developing a novel framework that extracts key biomechanical features (such as joint angles, limb velocities, and kinetic chain patterns) from motion data using Convolutional Neural Network Long Short-Term Memory (CNN-LSTM)-based models. These features are analyzed for relationships influencing stroke effectiveness and injury risk, forming the basis for feedback generation using large language models (LLMs). Leveraging the THETIS dataset and feature extraction techniques, our approach aims to produce feedback that is technically accurate, biomechanically grounded, and actionable for end-users. The experimental setup evaluates this framework on classification performance and interpretability, bridging the gap between explainable AI and sports biomechanics."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 4 figures, 2 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03921","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:01:48Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:01:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:01:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03914","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03914","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03914","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yonnel Chen Kuang","familyName":"Piao","name":"Piao, Yonnel Chen Kuang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jean Carlors","familyName":"Paul","name":"Paul, Jean Carlors","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leuson","familyName":"Da Silva","name":"Da Silva, Leuson","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arghavan Moradi","familyName":"Dakhel","name":"Dakhel, Arghavan Moradi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammad","familyName":"Hamdaqa","name":"Hamdaqa, Mohammad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Foutse","familyName":"Khomh","name":"Khomh, Foutse","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Refactoring with LLMs: Bridging Human Expertise and Machine Understanding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T19:40:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:41:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Code refactoring is a fundamental software engineering practice aimed at improving code quality and maintainability. Despite its importance, developers often neglect refactoring due to the significant time, effort, and resources it requires, as well as the lack of immediate functional rewards. Although several automated refactoring tools have been proposed, they remain limited in supporting a broad spectrum of refactoring types. In this study, we explore whether instruction strategies inspired by human best-practice guidelines can enhance the ability of Large Language Models (LLMs) to perform diverse refactoring tasks automatically. Leveraging the instruction-following and code comprehension capabilities of state-of-the-art LLMs (e.g., GPT-mini and DeepSeek-V3), we draw on Martin Fowler's refactoring guidelines to design multiple instruction strategies that encode motivations, procedural steps, and transformation objectives for 61 well-known refactoring types. We evaluate these strategies on benchmark examples and real-world code snippets from GitHub projects. Our results show that instruction designs grounded in Fowler's guidelines enable LLMs to successfully perform all benchmark refactoring types and preserve program semantics in real-world settings, an essential criterion for effective refactoring. Moreover, while descriptive instructions are more interpretable to humans, our results show that rule-based instructions often lead to better performance in specific scenarios. Interestingly, allowing models to focus on the overall goal of refactoring, rather than prescribing a fixed transformation type, can yield even greater improvements in code quality."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"43 pages, 2 figures, 9 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03914","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":1,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:01:38Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:01:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:01:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":134,"totalPages":2,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-10-06T16%3A00%3A00Z%20TO%202025-10-07T15%3A59%3A59Z%5D%20AND%20((agent%20OR%20agents%20OR%20RAG%20OR%20retrieval%20OR%20memory%20OR%20tool%20OR%20tools%20OR%20planning)%20AND%20(%22language%20model%22%20OR%20%22language%20models%22%20OR%20LLM%20OR%20LLMs))\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=1\u0026sort=registered","next":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=2\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-10-06T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-10-07T15%3A59%3A59Z%5D+AND+%28%28agent+OR+agents+OR+RAG+OR+retrieval+OR+memory+OR+tool+OR+tools+OR+planning%29+AND+%28%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+LLM+OR+LLMs%29%29"}}